+[{"u":"/en/systems/gearbox/","b":"Gearbox System — Reduction Stages, Oil Management and Diagnosis","h":"","t":"Technical reference ¶ Gearbox System ¶ The rotor turns at 6–15 rpm; a conventional generator wants 1000–1500 rpm. The gearbox bridges that gap, and in doing so it becomes the component that carries the highest torque in the machine — and the one that accounts for a large share of serious downtime."},{"u":"/en/systems/gearbox/","b":"Gearbox System — Reduction Stages, Oil Management and Diagnosis","h":"Why the gearbox is the critical component","t":"At the input the gearbox sees torque in the thousands of kilonewton-metres, delivered by a rotor that never turns at a constant load. Every gust, every tower passage and every yaw movement arrives as a load variation. A ratio in the region of 1:80 to 1:100 turns that slow, heavy input into fast, light output — and every stage of that conversion is a place where heat is generated and material fatigues. ¶ This is also why the gearless alternative exists. Removing the gearbox removes this entire category of failure, at the cost of a very large and heavy generator. That trade-off is what separates the Vestas and Enercon philosophies."},{"u":"/en/systems/gearbox/","b":"Gearbox System — Reduction Stages, Oil Management and Diagnosis","h":"Design types","t":"Parallel-axis: several stages of parallel shafts. Simple, robust, well understood. Heavier and noisier for the same ratio. Used on the Vestas V110 generation. ¶ Planetary (epicyclic): a central sun gear with planets in a ring. Much more compact and quieter for the same torque because load is shared across several meshes, but the individual meshes are highly stressed, which makes pitting the characteristic failure. Used on the V126. ¶ Hybrid: a planetary input stage feeding helical output stages. The usual arrangement on 4 MW-class machines such as the V136, where torque is high but package size matters."},{"u":"/en/systems/gearbox/","b":"Gearbox System — Reduction Stages, Oil Management and Diagnosis","h":"Oil: the system inside the system","t":"Gearbox oil does three jobs at once — it separates the gear surfaces, it carries heat away, and it carries wear debris to the filter. It is common to treat oil as a consumable; in practice it is a diagnostic instrument. Its temperature, colour, smell and metal content describe the health of everything it touches. ¶ Volume: roughly 500–600 L on a 2.5 MW machine, 700–800 L at 3 MW, around 1000 L on 4 MW-class boxes. ¶ Nominal temperature: typically 45–65 °C. Alarm around 75–80 °C, shutdown around 90–95 °C. ¶ The threshold that matters: above roughly 75 °C viscosity falls and oxidation accelerates, which degrades the oil and raises the temperature further — a self-reinforcing loop. ¶ Metal analysis: the concentration trend matters more than any single figure. A rising slope is a warning even while absolute values look acceptable."},{"u":"/en/systems/gearbox/","b":"Gearbox System — Reduction Stages, Oil Management and Diagnosis","h":"Cooling","t":"Passive systems rely on a finned radiator and natural convection; active systems add a pump and thermostatically controlled fan. Either way the failure mode is the same and it is mundane: a radiator blocked with dust or salt. In coastal and dusty sites radiator cleaning is one of the highest-return maintenance tasks there is."},{"u":"/en/systems/gearbox/","b":"Gearbox System — Reduction Stages, Oil Management and Diagnosis","h":"Reading temperature correctly","t":"Pattern | What it suggests | Where to look | ¶ Rise grows with load | Oil problem — level or condition | Level, leak path, oil state | ¶ Rise flat across load | Cooling problem | Radiator, fan, coolant level | ¶ Sudden step change | Sensor or circulation fault | Sensor resistance, pump, filter blockage | ¶ Slow climb over months | Progressive degradation | Metal trend, seal condition | ¶ A temperature reading without a load reference cannot be interpreted. This is the method used in Case 02."},{"u":"/en/systems/gearbox/","b":"Gearbox System — Reduction Stages, Oil Management and Diagnosis","h":"Typical faults","t":"Gear pitting: surface fatigue on the tooth flanks, characteristic of highly loaded planetary stages. First evidence is usually in the metal analysis, not in a temperature or noise change. ¶ Shaft seal leakage: the common cause of gradual oil loss, typically appearing after 8–12 years. Worked through in Case 02. ¶ Bearing damage: shows in vibration spectra as a narrow high-frequency peak long before it is audible. ¶ Filter blockage: reduces circulation and raises temperature; often mistaken for a cooling fault. ¶ Oil degradation: oxidised oil loses its load-carrying ability even at the correct level."},{"u":"/en/systems/gearbox/","b":"Gearbox System — Reduction Stages, Oil Management and Diagnosis","h":"Maintenance schedule","t":"Task | Interval | Note | ¶ Visual check for leaks and oil level | Monthly | A falling level is a leak until proven otherwise | ¶ Oil temperature trend at a fixed wind band | Monthly (from SCADA) | Pick a band and watch the slope | ¶ Oil sampling and metal analysis | 6–12 months | Trend matters more than the absolute value | ¶ Radiator cleaning | 6 months | More often at dusty or coastal sites | ¶ Oil and hydraulic filter change | 12–24 months | Larger oil volumes load filters faster | ¶ Vibration measurement | 12 months | Record with rotor speed so harmonics can be read | ¶ Internal borescope inspection | 24–36 months | Tooth flank and bearing condition | ¶ Related ¶ All systems ¶ Case 02: Gearbox Temperature Rise ¶ Vestas series — parallel-axis vs planetary ¶ Enercon series — the gearless alternative ¶ Fault diagnosis tree (Turkish)"},{"u":"/en/systems/generator/","b":"Generator and Converter — DFIG, Direct-Drive and Cooling","h":"","t":"Technical reference ¶ Generator and Converter System ¶ The system that converts mechanical torque into electrical power, and the electronics that make that power fit for the grid. Which generator a turbine uses determines almost everything else about its drivetrain — including whether it has a gearbox at all."},{"u":"/en/systems/generator/","b":"Generator and Converter — DFIG, Direct-Drive and Cooling","h":"Squirrel-cage induction — fixed speed","t":"The older approach, common through the 1980s and 1990s. The generator runs effectively at grid frequency with a few percent of slip. Simple, robust and cheap, but the rotor cannot vary its speed to follow the wind, so energy capture is poorer and every gust is transmitted into the drivetrain as a load rather than absorbed as a speed change. Requires a gearbox."},{"u":"/en/systems/generator/","b":"Generator and Converter — DFIG, Direct-Drive and Cooling","h":"Doubly-fed induction generator (DFIG) — the modern geared standard","t":"The stator connects directly to the grid; the rotor connects through a converter. Because only the rotor circuit passes through power electronics, the converter handles a fraction of total power rather than all of it, which keeps it smaller and cheaper than a full converter. In exchange the machine can vary its speed across roughly ±30% and track the optimum tip speed ratio. ¶ The trade-off is complexity. Diagnosis on a DFIG almost always means working with the converter, and converter faults are often intermittent and condition-dependent — as in Case 05."},{"u":"/en/systems/generator/","b":"Generator and Converter — DFIG, Direct-Drive and Cooling","h":"Direct-drive — the gearless alternative","t":"The rotor drives a many-poled annular generator directly, with no reduction stage. Speed is low (roughly 6–18 rpm) and the generator diameter is correspondingly large. There is no gear wear, no oil change and no reduction-stage failure; the cost is a very heavy generator that complicates transport and erection, and a shift of maintenance attention onto thermal management. This is the Enercon design philosophy."},{"u":"/en/systems/generator/","b":"Generator and Converter — DFIG, Direct-Drive and Cooling","h":"The converter","t":"Topology: AC–DC–AC, bidirectional, built around IGBT modules. ¶ Function: controls rotor current and phase so that stator output stays synchronised and within grid requirements, including reactive power support. ¶ Cooling: usually liquid, with a pump and fan in the kilowatt range. ¶ Protection: overcurrent, grid fault ride-through, soft start. ¶ Field reality: most converter cabinet faults that look electronic turn out to be thermal or mechanical — a blocked cooling path or a loosened power joint."},{"u":"/en/systems/generator/","b":"Generator and Converter — DFIG, Direct-Drive and Cooling","h":"Cooling and insulation","t":"Passive cooling uses finned radiators and natural convection; active cooling adds liquid circulation and thermostatically controlled fans, with a typical target of 50–65 °C nominal, alarm above 80 °C and shutdown above 95 °C. Insulation is the slower concern: windings age, absorb moisture, and their insulation resistance falls. A megohm test with a target above 10 MΩ is the standard check, and it is worth doing on schedule rather than after a fault."},{"u":"/en/systems/generator/","b":"Generator and Converter — DFIG, Direct-Drive and Cooling","h":"Typical faults","t":"High temperature: most often a blocked radiator or cooling duct. But sensor calibration drift produces an identical picture, which is why a physical reading comes first — see Case 04. ¶ Overcurrent and grid faults: converter trips that may be genuine grid events or, as often, a thermal or contact problem inside the cabinet. ¶ Insulation degradation: falling megohm readings and rising leakage current, driven by moisture and age. ¶ Bearing wear: changed sound and rising vibration, typically after several years of service. ¶ Grid interaction: harmonics and reactive power issues, more pronounced on weak grids."},{"u":"/en/systems/generator/","b":"Generator and Converter — DFIG, Direct-Drive and Cooling","h":"Maintenance schedule","t":"Task | Interval | Note | ¶ Fan and cooling circuit check | 3 months | Sound, current, coolant level | ¶ Radiator and cooling duct cleaning | 6 months | The primary job on direct-drive machines | ¶ Generator temperature trend | Monthly (from SCADA) | Read against load, not in isolation | ¶ Thermal scan under load | 12 months | Finds hot joints invisible on a stopped machine | ¶ Power connection torque check | 12–24 months | Converter and transformer joints | ¶ Megohm insulation test | 24 months | Target above 10 MΩ; sample must be dry | ¶ Converter diagnostic health check | 24 months | Requires OEM tooling | ¶ Generator bearing regreasing | ~60 months | Per OEM specification | ¶ Related ¶ All systems ¶ Case 04: Generator Temperature Alarm ¶ Case 05: Converter Overcurrent ¶ Enercon series — direct-drive ¶ Fault diagnosis tree (Turkish)"},{"u":"/en/systems/","b":"Wind Turbine Systems — Pitch, Yaw, Gearbox, Generator","h":"","t":"Technical reference ¶ Turbine Systems ¶ Knowing a turbine by its faults starts with knowing its systems one at a time. These four pages cover the systems my hands are in most often: what each one does, what its normal values are, what it looks like when it fails, and how often it needs attention. ¶ Every page follows the same order: what the system does, its components, how it works, normal operating values, typical faults and a maintenance table. If you already have a fault and want to work backwards from the symptom, the fault diagnosis tree gets you there faster; to see how a real case was actually solved step by step, go to the field notes. ¶ 01Pitch SystemThe mechanism that sets blade angle against the wind. Motor, gearbox, sensor and PID control; high-current and asymmetry faults. 02Yaw SystemTurns the nacelle into the wind. Motor, slewing bearing, ring gear, vane sensor; yaw hunting and bearing wear. 03GearboxSteps the slow rotor up to generator speed. Parallel-axis and planetary designs, oil management, temperature control. 04Generator and ConverterTurns mechanical torque into electricity. Induction, DFIG and direct-drive; converter, cooling and insulation."},{"u":"/en/systems/","b":"Wind Turbine Systems — Pitch, Yaw, Gearbox, Generator","h":"Which wind turbine system fails most often?","t":"In geared designs a significant share of downtime traces back to the gearbox and to oil management; the pitch system is the one that raises the most alarms. In direct-drive machines (Enercon) there is no gearbox at all, so that entire failure category disappears and generator thermal management takes its place as the main concern."},{"u":"/en/systems/","b":"Wind Turbine Systems — Pitch, Yaw, Gearbox, Generator","h":"What is the difference between pitch and yaw?","t":"Pitch rotates each blade about its own axis, which sets how much power the rotor takes from the wind and acts as the primary brake. Yaw rotates the whole nacelle horizontally to keep the rotor facing the wind. Pitch is power control; yaw is pointing."},{"u":"/en/systems/","b":"Wind Turbine Systems — Pitch, Yaw, Gearbox, Generator","h":"What gearbox oil temperature is normal?","t":"It varies with model and load; a typical nominal band is 45–65 °C. Above roughly 75 °C is usually an alarm and above 90–95 °C a shutdown. What matters is not a single reading but the trend of that temperature at the same wind speed over weeks."},{"u":"/en/systems/pitch/","b":"Pitch System — Blade Angle Control, Motor and Faults","h":"","t":"Technical reference ¶ Pitch System ¶ The mechanism that turns each blade about its own axis to set how much power the rotor takes from the wind. It is also the turbine's primary brake: feathering the blades is what stops a machine safely when everything else has failed."},{"u":"/en/systems/pitch/","b":"Pitch System — Blade Angle Control, Motor and Faults","h":"What the pitch system does","t":"Below rated wind the blades sit at a small angle to extract as much energy as possible. Above rated wind the controller turns them progressively into the wind — towards the \"feather\" position parallel to the flow — so the rotor sheds power and the machine stays within its design loads. The same movement, driven to full feather, is the aerodynamic brake. ¶ This is why the pitch system is safety-critical rather than merely operational. A turbine that cannot pitch cannot protect itself."},{"u":"/en/systems/pitch/","b":"Pitch System — Blade Angle Control, Motor and Faults","h":"Components","t":"Pitch motor: electric, one per blade on independent-pitch machines. Typical ratings run from 11–15 kW on smaller rotors to 22–25 kW on heavy 3 MW-class blades. ¶ Pitch gearbox: a high-ratio reduction (commonly in the 1:200 to 1:500 range) between motor and blade bearing, because the blade turns slowly against very large moments. ¶ Blade bearing: the slewing bearing the blade rotates on, carrying the full root moment. ¶ Position sensor: an encoder reporting actual blade angle back to the controller. ¶ Backup supply: battery or capacitor storage able to drive the blades to feather on loss of grid. This is the element that makes the aerodynamic brake dependable."},{"u":"/en/systems/pitch/","b":"Pitch System — Blade Angle Control, Motor and Faults","h":"How the control works","t":"The controller compares the measured output or rotor speed against target, computes an error, and commands a blade angle. The loop is usually PID: the proportional term reacts to the present error, the integral term removes steady offset, the derivative term damps the response. On modern machines each blade can be commanded separately, which lets the controller reduce asymmetric loading as well as regulate power. ¶ The practical consequence is that the controller only knows what the encoder tells it. If calibration drifts, the machine behaves confidently and incorrectly — which is exactly the failure described in Case 07."},{"u":"/en/systems/pitch/","b":"Pitch System — Blade Angle Control, Motor and Faults","h":"Normal operating values","t":"Wind condition | Typical blade angle | What the system is doing | ¶ Below cut-in | ~90° (feather) | Parked, no torque | ¶ Cut-in to rated | 0–3° | Maximising energy capture | ¶ At rated | 3–10° | Starting to shed power | ¶ Above rated | 10–30° | Holding rated output | ¶ Near cut-out | Increasing toward feather | Preparing to stop | ¶ Emergency stop | 90° as fast as the drive allows | Aerodynamic braking | ¶ Pitch rate matters as much as angle: typical maximum rates run 3–4°/s on lighter machines and 8–10°/s on larger ones."},{"u":"/en/systems/pitch/","b":"Pitch System — Blade Angle Control, Motor and Faults","h":"Typical faults","t":"High motor current: the motor working against mechanical resistance — poor lubrication, bearing drag, or gearbox trouble. Worked through in Case 01. ¶ Pitch asymmetry: one blade at a different angle from the others. Causes rotor imbalance, 1P vibration and lost production. Often a calibration problem rather than a mechanical one. ¶ Encoder drift: the reported angle and the real angle diverge. The system looks healthy because it believes its own measurement. ¶ Backup supply degradation: the most dangerous fault on the list, because it is silent until the moment it is needed. This is why backup capacity testing is not optional. ¶ Blade bearing wear: increasing play or friction at the root; shows up as rising motor current and, eventually, as noise."},{"u":"/en/systems/pitch/","b":"Pitch System — Blade Angle Control, Motor and Faults","h":"Maintenance schedule","t":"Task | Interval | Note | ¶ Motor current comparison across the three blades | Monthly (from SCADA) | Compare blades against each other, not against the alarm limit | ¶ Visual check for oil traces on the pitch gearbox | Monthly | The cheapest early warning available | ¶ Blade bearing lubrication | 12 months | Check purged grease for metal | ¶ Backup supply capacity test | 12 months | Safety function — never skipped | ¶ Physical verification of pitch angle against encoder | 12–24 months | Catches calibration drift before it causes vibration | ¶ Pitch gearbox oil check or change | 24 months | Per OEM specification | ¶ Related ¶ All systems ¶ Case 01: Pitch Motor High Current ¶ Case 07: Blade Vibration ¶ Turbine model guide ¶ Fault diagnosis tree (Turkish)"},{"u":"/en/systems/yaw/","b":"Yaw System — Nacelle Orientation, Slewing Bearing and Faults","h":"","t":"Technical reference ¶ Yaw System ¶ The system that rotates the whole nacelle horizontally to keep the rotor facing the wind. It moves rarely and slowly, which is exactly why its problems tend to go unnoticed for months."},{"u":"/en/systems/yaw/","b":"Yaw System — Nacelle Orientation, Slewing Bearing and Faults","h":"What the yaw system does","t":"A rotor that is not facing the wind loses power roughly with the cosine of the misalignment angle, and it picks up asymmetric loading at the same time. The yaw system exists to keep that error small. It also manages cable twist: the nacelle can only rotate so many turns in one direction before the power cables below must be untwisted."},{"u":"/en/systems/yaw/","b":"Yaw System — Nacelle Orientation, Slewing Bearing and Faults","h":"Components","t":"Yaw motors: several electric drives working together, typically 3–5 kW each on smaller machines and 10–15 kW on large ones. ¶ Slewing bearing: a large-diameter ring bearing (roughly 3000–5000 mm) between tower top and nacelle, carrying several hundred tonnes of radial and moment load. ¶ Ring gear: the toothed ring the drive pinions engage, with a high overall reduction. ¶ Yaw brakes: hold the nacelle in position between movements so the drive is not fighting the wind continuously. ¶ Vane sensor: the wind direction sensor on the nacelle roof, typically accurate to a few degrees. ¶ Lubrication system: often automatic, feeding grease to distributed points around the bearing."},{"u":"/en/systems/yaw/","b":"Yaw System — Nacelle Orientation, Slewing Bearing and Faults","h":"How the control works","t":"The controller does not chase every gust. It averages wind direction over a window, and only commands a movement when the error exceeds a threshold and persists. That deliberate sluggishness is a design feature: a yaw system that reacted quickly would wear itself out and would never settle. ¶ When that balance is upset — by a noisy sensor, a dragging bearing, or a controller gain that is too high — the loop becomes unstable and the nacelle hunts. That combination is the subject of Case 03."},{"u":"/en/systems/yaw/","b":"Yaw System — Nacelle Orientation, Slewing Bearing and Faults","h":"Normal operating values","t":"Parameter | Typical value | Comment | ¶ Yaw rate | 0.3–0.8 °/s | Deliberately slow | ¶ Alignment tolerance | ±1° to ±5° | Tighter on newer machines | ¶ Movements per day | Tens, site dependent | A sharp rise suggests instability | ¶ Cable twist limit | ±2 to ±3 turns | Triggers an untwist cycle | ¶ Motor current | Steady, position independent | Variation with position means drag |"},{"u":"/en/systems/yaw/","b":"Yaw System — Nacelle Orientation, Slewing Bearing and Faults","h":"Typical faults","t":"Yaw hunting: the nacelle oscillates instead of settling. Almost always a combination of sensor noise, mechanical drag and controller gain — see Case 03. ¶ Bearing wear: often starts in one sector rather than around the whole ring, and the usual reason is a blocked lubrication line. Caught without an alarm in Case 06. ¶ Vane sensor faults: loose mounting, icing or drift. Everything downstream inherits the error. ¶ Brake problems: worn pads or residual pressure make the drives work against the brake continuously, raising current and heat. ¶ Ring gear damage: usually a consequence of prolonged bearing trouble rather than a primary fault."},{"u":"/en/systems/yaw/","b":"Yaw System — Nacelle Orientation, Slewing Bearing and Faults","h":"Maintenance schedule","t":"Task | Interval | Note | ¶ Yaw motor current trend (peaks, not just averages) | Monthly (from SCADA) | Rising peaks are the earliest sign of drag | ¶ Vane sensor mounting and alignment | 6 months | One minute; poisons everything if loose | ¶ Slewing bearing lubrication | 12 months | Collect purged grease point by point and inspect for metal | ¶ Automatic lubrication line verification | 12 months | A running pump is not proof of delivery | ¶ Ring gear backlash measurement | 12–24 months | Shows whether wear has spread from bearing to gear | ¶ Yaw brake inspection | 12–24 months | Pad thickness and residual pressure | ¶ Related ¶ All systems ¶ Case 03: Yaw Hunting ¶ Case 06: Yaw Bearing Wear ¶ Enercon series ¶ Fault diagnosis tree (Turkish)"},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e70/","b":"Enercon E-70 — Technical Guide","h":"","t":"Model guide ¶ Enercon E-70 E4 ¶ Enercon’s gearless 2 MW-class model: 2.3 MW rated power, 71 m rotor. The rotor drives a multi-pole synchronous annular generator directly; there is no gearbox in between. In the field, attention moves from the gearbox to the generator, the pitch system and the converter."},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e70/","b":"Enercon E-70 — Technical Guide","h":"What defines this model","t":"In the Enercon design the inner ring of the generator turns together with the rotor. Speed is low (6–21 rpm), so the generator is multi-pole and large in diameter. The variable-frequency power is rectified and converted to grid frequency by the Enercon converter. ¶ Without a gearbox, gear oil changes, oil sampling and gearbox bearing failures drop off the list. Generator cooling, the pitch system’s emergency supply and the converter modules take their place."},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e70/","b":"Enercon E-70 — Technical Guide","h":"Key technical data","t":"Parameter | Value | ¶ Rated power | 2.3 MW | ¶ Rotor diameter | 71 m | ¶ Hub height | 64–113 m | ¶ Rotor speed | 6–21 rpm (variable) | ¶ Cut-in | 2.5 m/s | ¶ Storm control | Power reduced gradually between 28 and 34 m/s | ¶ Generator | Direct-drive synchronous annular generator | ¶ Grid feed | Enercon converter (rectifier, DC link, inverter) | ¶ Pitch | Independent system per blade with emergency supply | ¶ Brakes | Aerodynamic (pitch), rotor brake, rotor lock |"},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e70/","b":"Enercon E-70 — Technical Guide","h":"Annular generator","t":"Large-diameter and multi-pole; heat and clean air paths are the most critical periodic items in this design. On dusty and salty sites a clogged air path comes straight back as a temperature warning."},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e70/","b":"Enercon E-70 — Technical Guide","h":"Storm control","t":"Classic designs stop the turbine completely at a set speed. Enercon reduces speed and power gradually as the wind rises, so the turbine keeps producing partially in high wind. ¶ Low power in high wind can look like a fault; the control system may simply be working as designed."},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e70/","b":"Enercon E-70 — Technical Guide","h":"Pitch and emergency supply","t":"Each blade has its own pitch system and emergency energy source. The capacity test of that source, which feathers the blades on grid loss, should never be skipped."},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e70/","b":"Enercon E-70 — Technical Guide","h":"E-70 and E-82 (Enercon)","t":"Item | E-70 E4 | E-82 E2 | ¶ Rated power | 2.3 MW | 2.0 / 2.3 MW | ¶ Rotor diameter | 71 m | 82 m | ¶ Rotor speed | 6–21 rpm | 6–19.5 rpm | ¶ Drivetrain | Gearless, annular generator | Gearless, annular generator |"},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e70/","b":"Enercon E-70 — Technical Guide","h":"Maintenance topics that matter in the field","t":"A slow rise in generator temperature: check the cooling air paths and filters first. ¶ Pitch emergency supply: capacity test and connections. ¶ Yaw: adjustment gears and lubrication; the first place to look for hunting and noise. ¶ Converter modules and cooling: read temperature and fault logs together under load. ¶ About this page ¶ Technical data come from documents published by the manufacturer and can change with variant and site configuration. The maintenance topics summarise general field practice; for intervals and torque values the manufacturer’s service documentation governs. My first role at Enercon (2013–2016) was steel–concrete hybrid tower assembly; this page is a model summary built on verified data, not a maintenance technician’s personal log. ¶ Soner SoyluWind Turbine Field Service Technician — Enercon, Nordex, Vestas · GWO certified"},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e70/","b":"Enercon E-70 — Technical Guide","h":"Sources","t":"ENERCON E-70 E4 teknik veri (BAPE dosyası, PR1) ¶ Warning: Every job in the nacelle, hub and tower follows lockout–tagout (LOTO), rotor lock and the manufacturer’s safety instructions. This page is field experience and published data; it does not replace the service documentation. ¶ Related ¶ All turbine models ¶ Turbine systems ¶ Field notes — real faults ¶ Fault diagnosis tree (Turkish)"},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e82/","b":"Enercon E-82 — Technical Guide","h":"","t":"Model guide ¶ Enercon E-82 ¶ The gearless Enercon model with an 82 m rotor. It was built at several ratings: E-82 E2 at 2.0 and 2.3 MW, E-82 E4 in the 3.0 MW class. The same rotor comes with different generator and control configurations, so in the field “E-82” needs the variant spelled out."},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e82/","b":"Enercon E-82 — Technical Guide","h":"What defines this model","t":"The rotor drives a multi-pole synchronous annular generator directly. Speed is low (6–19.5 rpm on the E2) and the generator diameter is large. With no gearbox there are no oil changes, gear pitting or gearbox bearings on the list. ¶ The price is weight and heat: the annular generator is heavy and large, and its transport and lift need special planning. Maintenance attention moves from the gearbox to generator cooling and the pitch system’s emergency supply."},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e82/","b":"Enercon E-82 — Technical Guide","h":"Key technical data","t":"Parameter | Value | ¶ Rated power | 2.0 / 2.3 MW (E2), 3.0 MW (E4) | ¶ Rotor diameter | 82 m | ¶ Hub height | 70–138 m | ¶ Rotor speed (E2) | 6–19.5 rpm (variable) | ¶ Storm control | Power reduced gradually between 28 and 34 m/s | ¶ Generator | Direct-drive synchronous annular generator | ¶ Grid feed | Enercon converter | ¶ Pitch | Independent system per blade with emergency supply | ¶ Brakes | Three independent pitch systems, rotor brake, rotor lock | ¶ Yaw | Adjustment gears, load-dependent damping |"},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e82/","b":"Enercon E-82 — Technical Guide","h":"Annular generator","t":"Keeping the cooling air paths open is the most critical periodic job in this design; a clogged path comes straight back as a temperature warning. A case walking through a similar mechanism: generator temperature alarm."},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e82/","b":"Enercon E-82 — Technical Guide","h":"Storm control","t":"Speed and power are reduced gradually as the wind rises — a soft transition instead of a hard cut-out. Low power in high wind can look like a fault. ¶ With fewer stop–start cycles, structural fatigue drops as well."},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e82/","b":"Enercon E-82 — Technical Guide","h":"Pitch and redundancy","t":"Each blade is controlled by its own drive, with an emergency source to feather the blades on grid loss. In a gearless design the aerodynamic brake is the primary safety, so testing that source is critical."},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e82/","b":"Enercon E-82 — Technical Guide","h":"E-82 and E-92 (Enercon)","t":"Item | E-82 E2 | E-92 | ¶ Rated power | 2.0 / 2.3 MW | 2.35 MW | ¶ Rotor diameter | 82 m | 92 m | ¶ Drivetrain | Gearless | Gearless |"},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e82/","b":"Enercon E-82 — Technical Guide","h":"Maintenance topics that matter in the field","t":"Generator temperature: cooling air paths and filters. ¶ Pitch emergency supply: capacity test. ¶ Yaw: adjustment gears and lubrication; the first place to look for hunting. Case: yaw hunting. ¶ Converter modules and their cooling. ¶ About this page ¶ Technical data come from documents published by the manufacturer and change by variant. The maintenance topics summarise general field practice; for intervals and torque values the manufacturer’s service documentation governs. ¶ Soner SoyluWind Turbine Field Service Technician — Enercon, Nordex, Vestas · GWO certified"},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e82/","b":"Enercon E-82 — Technical Guide","h":"Sources","t":"ENERCON E-82 ürün bilgisi (PDF) ¶ Warning: Every job in the nacelle, hub and tower follows lockout–tagout (LOTO), rotor lock and the manufacturer’s safety instructions. This page is field experience and published data; it does not replace the service documentation. ¶ Related ¶ All turbine models ¶ Turbine systems ¶ Field notes — real faults ¶ Fault diagnosis tree (Turkish)"},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e92/","b":"Enercon E-92 — Technical Guide","h":"","t":"Model guide ¶ Enercon E-92 ¶ 2.35 MW rated power, 92 m rotor. A larger rotor but a lower rating than the E-82 — that choice explains the whole E-92: it was designed to stay in production for more of the year on low- and medium-wind sites."},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e92/","b":"Enercon E-92 — Technical Guide","h":"Why a larger rotor and a smaller rating?","t":"Rated power is the turbine’s ceiling, reached only in strong enough wind. On a weak-wind site the turbine spends most of its time below it. What counts there is not a high ceiling but useful power in light wind. ¶ A larger rotor collects more energy from the same weak wind; a lower rating keeps the generator and power electronics at a point they reach early. The result is an earlier start and a high capacity factor. On a strong-wind site the same choice wastes potential."},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e92/","b":"Enercon E-92 — Technical Guide","h":"Key technical data","t":"Parameter | Value | ¶ Rated power | 2.35 MW | ¶ Rotor diameter | 92 m | ¶ Hub height | 78–138 m | ¶ Rotor speed | up to 16 rpm | ¶ Cut-in | 2 m/s | ¶ Rated wind speed | 13 m/s | ¶ Drivetrain | Gearless, direct drive | ¶ Generator | Synchronous, wound | ¶ Nacelle mass | 64 t | ¶ Rotor + hub mass | 41 t | ¶ Wind class | IEC IIA |"},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e92/","b":"Enercon E-92 — Technical Guide","h":"Annular generator and heat","t":"A turbine that stays in production for long hours in light wind keeps its generator cooling working constantly; clean air paths are a first-order concern."},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e92/","b":"Enercon E-92 — Technical Guide","h":"Yaw","t":"A larger rotor means a higher moment on the yaw system; lubrication of the adjustment gears and the damping are followed."},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e92/","b":"Enercon E-92 — Technical Guide","h":"E-82 and E-92 (Enercon)","t":"Item | E-82 E2 | E-92 | ¶ Rated power | 2.0 / 2.3 MW | 2.35 MW | ¶ Rotor diameter | 82 m | 92 m | ¶ Rotor speed | 6–19.5 rpm | up to 16 rpm |"},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e92/","b":"Enercon E-92 — Technical Guide","h":"Maintenance topics that matter in the field","t":"Generator cooling air paths and filters. ¶ Pitch emergency supply and connections. ¶ Yaw adjustment gears and lubrication. ¶ Blade surface and root connections: periodic visual checks on a long blade. ¶ About this page ¶ Technical data come from published data sheets and can change with variant and site configuration. For intervals and torque values the manufacturer’s service documentation governs. ¶ Soner SoyluWind Turbine Field Service Technician — Enercon, Nordex, Vestas · GWO certified"},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/e92/","b":"Enercon E-92 — Technical Guide","h":"Sources","t":"The Wind Power — Enercon E92/2350 ana veriler ¶ Warning: Every job in the nacelle, hub and tower follows lockout–tagout (LOTO), rotor lock and the manufacturer’s safety instructions. This page is field experience and published data; it does not replace the service documentation. ¶ Related ¶ All turbine models ¶ Turbine systems ¶ Field notes — real faults ¶ Fault diagnosis tree (Turkish)"},{"u":"/en/turbines/enercon/","b":"Enercon E70, E82, E92 — Direct-Drive Guide","h":"","t":"Manufacturer ¶ Enercon Series ¶ Enercon's design signature is that it uses no gearbox. The rotor drives an annular generator directly. That removes the single largest failure category in the industry; in exchange it brings a very heavy generator and a sensitivity to thermal management. ¶ The field consequence of the gearless design is plain: there is no oil change, no gear pitting and no reduction-stage failure on the maintenance list. What replaces them is the generator cooling ducts — a blocked duct comes straight back as a temperature alarm. ¶ 2.3 MWEnercon E7071 m rotor, direct-drive annular (synchronous) generator, no gearbox. From 2013 to 2016 I worked on hybrid tower assembly on Enercon sites. 2.0–3.0 MWEnercon E8282 m rotor, direct-drive. E2 and E4 variants, storm control, annular generator maintenance. 2.35 MWEnercon E9292 m rotor, low-wind design (IEC IIA). A large rotor with a moderate rating for early power in light wind."},{"u":"/en/turbines/","b":"Wind Turbine Models — Vestas, Enercon, Nordex","h":"","t":"Model guide ¶ Turbine Models ¶ Turbines that do the same job behave very differently. A geared Vestas and a gearless Enercon do not share a maintenance calendar or a fault list. These pages compare the models you meet in the field using manufacturer-published data and field maintenance topics. ¶ Each model page carries sourced specifications, a comparison table against its sibling model, model-specific systems and the maintenance topics that matter in the field. For how the systems work in general, see turbine systems."},{"u":"/en/turbines/","b":"Wind Turbine Models — Vestas, Enercon, Nordex","h":"By manufacturer","t":"VestasVestas SeriesV110, V126 and V136. Geared; the V110 with a doubly-fed generator, the 4 MW platform with a full-scale converter. EnerconEnercon SeriesE70, E82 and E92. Gearless direct-drive, annular generator, storm control. NordexNordex N117 DeltaA 15-stop tour of a 3 MW turbine from the tower door to the blade tip. What you see and what goes wrong at each stop. NordexNordex N902.5 MW class, common across Türkiye. Interactive 3D model of the seven assembly stages."},{"u":"/en/turbines/","b":"Wind Turbine Models — Vestas, Enercon, Nordex","h":"Is a gearless (direct-drive) turbine better?","t":"For maintenance, yes: with no gearbox there is no gear wear, no oil change and no reduction-stage failure. In exchange the annular generator is very heavy, which makes transport and erection harder and raises the initial cost, and generator thermal management becomes the main thing you watch."},{"u":"/en/turbines/","b":"Wind Turbine Models — Vestas, Enercon, Nordex","h":"How long does it take to erect a wind turbine?","t":"It varies a lot with site conditions, tower type and the crane plan, so a single number would mislead. What drives it most is the number and height of tower sections, blade length, crane rig-up and the days lost waiting out the wind limit. On sites with concrete or hybrid towers, building the tower is a separate job on top."},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/","b":"Vestas V110, V126, V136 — Technical Guide","h":"","t":"Manufacturer ¶ Vestas Series ¶ All three Vestas models in this guide are geared. The V110-2.0 MW is the low-wind member of the 2 MW platform: a doubly-fed (slip-ring) generator with a partial-power converter. The V126-3.45 MW and V136-4.2 MW belong to the 4 MW platform and use a full-scale converter. ¶ The most decisive difference across the series is the drivetrain layout: the V110 has one planetary and two helical stages, the 4 MW platform two planetary and one helical stage. That changes weight, the size of the oil system and the symptoms you follow in the field. Every specification on these pages comes from manufacturer-published documents, with sources listed. ¶ 2.0 MWVestas V110110 m rotor, 1 planetary + 2 helical stages, doubly-fed generator. Low-wind class (IEC IIIA). 3.45 MWVestas V126126 m rotor, 2 planetary + 1 helical stage, full-scale converter. 4 MW platform. 4.2 MWVestas V136136 m rotor, 66.7 m blade, full-scale converter. In 2020 I worked on ground and nacelle installation on a V136-3.45 MW project."},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v110/","b":"Vestas V110-2.0 MW — Technical Guide","h":"","t":"Model guide ¶ Vestas V110-2.0 MW ¶ The large-rotor member of Vestas’ 2 MW platform for low-wind sites: 2.0 MW rated power, 110 m rotor, 54 m blades. A classic geared architecture with a doubly-fed (slip-ring) generator; in the field most of the time goes into the gearbox oil system, the hydraulic pitch and the generator slip-ring side."},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v110/","b":"Vestas V110-2.0 MW — Technical Guide","h":"What defines this model","t":"The V110 grows the rotor instead of the rating. A 2.0 MW ceiling on a 110 m rotor gives a turbine that reaches useful power early in light wind and targets a high capacity factor. Wind class IEC IIIA. ¶ The architecture is familiar: rotor → main shaft → gearbox → high-speed shaft → doubly-fed generator. The stator connects directly to the grid, the rotor through slip rings to a partial-power converter. Each blade is pitched by its own hydraulic cylinder."},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v110/","b":"Vestas V110-2.0 MW — Technical Guide","h":"Key technical data","t":"Parameter | Value | ¶ Rated power | 2.0 MW | ¶ Rotor diameter | 110 m | ¶ Blade length | 54 m | ¶ Hub height | 80 / 95 / 125 m (by grid frequency and package) | ¶ Cut-in | 3 m/s | ¶ Rated wind speed | 11.5 m/s | ¶ Cut-out | 20 m/s | ¶ Wind class | IEC IIIA | ¶ Gearbox | 1 planetary + 2 helical stages | ¶ Generator | Doubly-fed with slip rings; 4-pole (50 Hz) | ¶ Pitch | Full-blade feathering, one pitch cylinder per blade | ¶ Tower | Tubular steel |"},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v110/","b":"Vestas V110-2.0 MW — Technical Guide","h":"Gearbox and oil system","t":"One planetary and two helical stages step the main-shaft speed up to generator speed. ¶ What gets checked most: oil level and temperature trend, filter differential pressure, oil samples (metals and water), seals, and vibration trends for the bearings. ¶ A temperature that drifts up at the same wind speed matters more than any single absolute value."},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v110/","b":"Vestas V110-2.0 MW — Technical Guide","h":"Doubly-fed generator (DFIG)","t":"The rotor winding reaches the converter through slip rings and brushes, so brush wear, brush dust and slip-ring surface are periodic checks. ¶ Because the stator is tied directly to the grid, grid events (voltage dips, imbalance) can come back as generator or converter faults."},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v110/","b":"Vestas V110-2.0 MW — Technical Guide","h":"Hydraulic pitch","t":"A pitch cylinder turns each blade; pressure, accumulator pre-charge and hose and fitting tightness decide the health of the system. ¶ The accumulators hold the energy to feather the blades on grid loss; testing them is a safety item that should never be skipped."},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v110/","b":"Vestas V110-2.0 MW — Technical Guide","h":"V110 and V126 (Vestas)","t":"Item | V110-2.0 MW | V126-3.45 MW | ¶ Rated power | 2.0 MW | 3.45 MW | ¶ Rotor diameter | 110 m | 126 m | ¶ Gearbox | 1 planetary + 2 helical | 2 planetary + 1 helical | ¶ Generator / converter | Doubly-fed, partial converter | Full-scale converter | ¶ Pitch | Hydraulic cylinder | Hydraulic cylinder |"},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v110/","b":"Vestas V110-2.0 MW — Technical Guide","h":"Maintenance topics that matter in the field","t":"Hydraulic leaks in the hub and nacelle: a small drip becomes a dirty hub and an out-of-spec accumulator over time. ¶ A slow rise in gearbox oil temperature: filter, cooler radiator and oil level are checked together. ¶ Brush dust in the generator slip-ring compartment: cleaning and brush-length measurement are routine. ¶ Yaw brake pads and gear lubrication: the first place to look for hunting and noise complaints. ¶ About this page ¶ Technical data come from documents published by the manufacturer and can change with variant, software package and site configuration. The maintenance topics summarise general field practice; for intervals and torque values the manufacturer’s service documentation always governs. ¶ Soner SoyluWind Turbine Field Service Technician — Enercon, Nordex, Vestas · GWO certified"},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v110/","b":"Vestas V110-2.0 MW — Technical Guide","h":"Sources","t":"Vestas — V110-2.0 MW ürün sayfası ¶ EDP Arkwright Summit — V110 çizim ve teknik özellikler (Exhibit 7) ¶ Warning: Every job in the nacelle, hub and tower follows lockout–tagout (LOTO), rotor lock and the manufacturer’s safety instructions. This page is field experience and published data; it does not replace the service documentation. ¶ Related ¶ All turbine models ¶ Turbine systems ¶ Field notes — real faults ¶ Fault diagnosis tree (Turkish)"},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v126/","b":"Vestas V126-3.45 MW — Technical Guide","h":"","t":"Model guide ¶ Vestas V126-3.45 MW ¶ Vestas’ 3 MW-class (today’s 4 MW platform) medium-wind model. The first version was 3.3 MW, the common one is 3.45 MW; the rotor is 126 m. Unlike the V110 it uses a full-scale converter, so all the power passes through the converter."},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v126/","b":"Vestas V126-3.45 MW — Technical Guide","h":"What defines this model","t":"The V126 was designed for medium-wind, high-turbulence sites (IEC IIIA / IIB). Its 12,469 m² swept area catches roughly a third more wind than the V110. ¶ The drivetrain uses a gearbox with two planetary stages and one helical stage. The full-scale converter decouples the generator from the grid: grid events reach the generator less, while converter cooling and power-electronics maintenance move to the centre of the work."},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v126/","b":"Vestas V126-3.45 MW — Technical Guide","h":"Key technical data","t":"Parameter | Value | ¶ Rated power | 3.3 MW (first version) / 3.45 MW | ¶ Rotor diameter | 126 m | ¶ Swept area | 12,469 m² | ¶ Hub height (3.45 MW) | 87 / 117 / 137 / 147 / 149 / 166 m | ¶ Cut-in | 3 m/s | ¶ Cut-out | 22.5 m/s | ¶ Wind class | IEC IIIA / IEC IIB | ¶ Gearbox | 2 planetary + 1 helical stage | ¶ Converter | Full scale | ¶ Pitch | Full-blade feathering, one pitch cylinder per blade |"},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v126/","b":"Vestas V126-3.45 MW — Technical Guide","h":"Gearbox","t":"Two planetary stages carry high torque in a compact housing; planet gear and bearing condition are followed through oil analysis and vibration trends. ¶ Filter differential pressure, oil temperature and cooler cleanliness are read together."},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v126/","b":"Vestas V126-3.45 MW — Technical Guide","h":"Full-scale converter","t":"Because all the power passes through the converter, its cooling circuit (fluid level, pump, fan, radiator) and power-connection torque are critical maintenance items. ¶ A thermal scan under load catches loose connections that never show on a stopped turbine."},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v126/","b":"Vestas V126-3.45 MW — Technical Guide","h":"V126 and V136 (Vestas)","t":"Item | V126-3.45 MW | V136-4.2 MW | ¶ Rotor diameter | 126 m | 136 m | ¶ Swept area | 12,469 m² | 14,527 m² | ¶ Gearbox | 2 planetary + 1 helical | 2 planetary + 1 helical | ¶ Converter | Full scale | Full scale |"},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v126/","b":"Vestas V126-3.45 MW — Technical Guide","h":"Maintenance topics that matter in the field","t":"Converter cooling: a dirty radiator or low fluid level is a common cause of temperature and overcurrent faults at high power. ¶ Gearbox oil temperature and filter alarms: trend tracking and oil samples. ¶ Hydraulic leaks and accumulator pre-charge. ¶ Blade bearings and root bolts: periodic checks and lubrication. ¶ About this page ¶ Technical data come from documents published by the manufacturer and can change with variant, software package and site configuration. Generator type and some internal layout details differ between versions and are therefore not given here. For intervals and torque values the manufacturer’s service documentation governs. ¶ Soner SoyluWind Turbine Field Service Technician — Enercon, Nordex, Vestas · GWO certified"},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v126/","b":"Vestas V126-3.45 MW — Technical Guide","h":"Sources","t":"Vestas — V126-3.45 MW ürün sayfası ¶ Warning: Every job in the nacelle, hub and tower follows lockout–tagout (LOTO), rotor lock and the manufacturer’s safety instructions. This page is field experience and published data; it does not replace the service documentation. ¶ Related ¶ All turbine models ¶ Turbine systems ¶ Field notes — real faults ¶ Fault diagnosis tree (Turkish)"},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v136/","b":"Vestas V136 — Technical Guide (3.45 and 4.2 MW)","h":"","t":"Model guide ¶ Vestas V136 ¶ The large-rotor, low-to-medium-wind model of Vestas’ 4 MW platform: 136 m rotor, 66.7 m blades. The first common version was 3.45 MW, the later one 4.0–4.2 MW. In 2020 I worked on ground and nacelle assembly on a V136-3.45 MW project; the field notes on this page come from that job."},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v136/","b":"Vestas V136 — Technical Guide (3.45 and 4.2 MW)","h":"What defines this model","t":"The energy a turbine captures grows linearly with swept area. The V136’s 14,527 m² is about 17% more than the V126 — on a weak-wind site that area is the biggest lever you have. ¶ The price is paid in the blade: a 66.7 m blade is long, heavy and flexible. Root moment, blade-bearing load, transport and the crane plan all grow with it. The drivetrain follows the rest of the platform: a gearbox with two planetary and one helical stage and a full-scale converter."},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v136/","b":"Vestas V136 — Technical Guide (3.45 and 4.2 MW)","h":"Key technical data","t":"Parameter | Value | ¶ Rated power | 3.45 MW / 4.0–4.2 MW (by version) | ¶ Rotor diameter | 136 m | ¶ Swept area | 14,527 m² | ¶ Blade length | 66.7 m | ¶ Hub height (4.2 MW) | 105 / 112 / 117 / 149 / 166 m | ¶ Cut-in | 3 m/s | ¶ Cut-out (4.2 MW) | 25 m/s | ¶ Wind class (4.2 MW) | IEC IIB / IEC S | ¶ Gearbox | 2 planetary + 1 helical stage | ¶ Converter | Full scale | ¶ Power regulation | Pitch regulated, variable speed |"},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v136/","b":"Vestas V136 — Technical Guide (3.45 and 4.2 MW)","h":"Blade and blade bearing","t":"On a long blade the pre-tension of root bolts and blade-bearing lubrication head the periodic work. ¶ Even a few degrees of pitch difference between blades comes back as rotor imbalance and vibration; the physical angle is compared with the encoder value."},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v136/","b":"Vestas V136 — Technical Guide (3.45 and 4.2 MW)","h":"Assembly","t":"Nacelle and blade lift windows are very sensitive to wind limits; it is usually the hours spent waiting on wind, not the mechanical work, that sets the assembly schedule. ¶ Torque and tensioning sequences on flange and root connections follow the documentation and are marked; later checks rely on those marks."},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v136/","b":"Vestas V136 — Technical Guide (3.45 and 4.2 MW)","h":"Full-scale converter and cooling","t":"All the power passes through the converter; its liquid cooling circuit and power connections are critical maintenance items."},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v136/","b":"Vestas V136 — Technical Guide (3.45 and 4.2 MW)","h":"V126 and V136 (Vestas)","t":"Item | V126-3.45 MW | V136-4.2 MW | ¶ Rotor diameter | 126 m | 136 m | ¶ Swept area | 12,469 m² | 14,527 m² | ¶ Blade length | — | 66.7 m | ¶ Converter | Full scale | Full scale |"},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v136/","b":"Vestas V136 — Technical Guide (3.45 and 4.2 MW)","h":"Maintenance topics that matter in the field","t":"On a vibration complaint, verify the pitch angles physically first; look at calibration before blaming the blade. ¶ Blade-to-tower clearance and blade surface: periodic visual checks matter more on a long blade. ¶ Converter and gearbox coolers: on a dusty site, radiator cleaning is one of the highest-return jobs. ¶ About this page ¶ Technical data come from documents published by the manufacturer; hub heights, wind class and cut-out differ between the 3.45 MW and 4.2 MW versions. For intervals and torque values the manufacturer’s service documentation governs. ¶ Soner SoyluWind Turbine Field Service Technician — Enercon, Nordex, Vestas · GWO certified"},{"u":"/en/turbines/vestas/v136/","b":"Vestas V136 — Technical Guide (3.45 and 4.2 MW)","h":"Sources","t":"Vestas — V136-4.2 MW ürün sayfası ¶ Warning: Every job in the nacelle, hub and tower follows lockout–tagout (LOTO), rotor lock and the manufacturer’s safety instructions. This page is field experience and published data; it does not replace the service documentation. ¶ Related ¶ All turbine models ¶ Turbine systems ¶ Field notes — real faults ¶ Fault diagnosis tree (Turkish)"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/blade-vibration/","b":"Blade Vibration — 1P/3P Harmonics and Pitch Asymmetry","h":"","t":"From the field · Case 07 ¶ Blade Vibration — It Was the Calibration, Not the Blade ¶ A Vestas V126. The operator called saying \"one of the blades must be damaged\"; the vibration became noticeable at a particular wind speed. A blade inspection is an expensive job, so before touching the blade I wanted to look at the vibration itself. Just as well — there was nothing wrong with the blades."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/blade-vibration/","b":"Blade Vibration — 1P/3P Harmonics and Pitch Asymmetry","h":"Symptom","t":"Complaint: noticeable vibration in the nacelle, audible from the tower base as well ¶ Condition: only in the 7–9 m/s wind band. Below and above it, vibration is normal. ¶ SCADA: the vibration value rises in that band but does not reach the alarm threshold ¶ Production: the power curve sits slightly below expectation in that band ¶ Visual: no visible damage, cracking or surface defect on the blades through binoculars from the ground"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/blade-vibration/","b":"Blade Vibration — 1P/3P Harmonics and Pitch Asymmetry","h":"First assessment","t":"The single most important clue was that the vibration was confined to a narrow wind band. If there were structural damage in a blade, you would not expect its effect to appear only in that band; a damaged blade causes more trouble as the wind rises. ¶ Vibration that appears in a narrow band is the signature of resonance: at a particular rotor speed the forcing frequency approaches a natural frequency of the structure and the vibration grows. But resonance is not a cause on its own — something has to be feeding it. What I was looking for was an imbalance giving the rotor one push per revolution. ¶ What are 1P and 3P? The rotor's own rotational frequency is called 1P: however many turns per second the rotor makes, that is the frequency. On a three-bladed rotor the blades pass the tower at three times that rate, which is 3P. The distinction is central to the diagnosis: a rise in 3P points to tower interaction and aerodynamics, while a rise in 1P says the rotor is unbalanced — one of the three blades is behaving differently from the others."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/blade-vibration/","b":"Blade Vibration — 1P/3P Harmonics and Pitch Asymmetry","h":"Step-by-step diagnosis","t":"Recorded the vibration data together with rotor speed. Looking only at the amplitude of the vibration is not enough. I recorded rotor speed at the same time so that I could read the frequency in terms of 1P and 3P. ¶ Produced the spectrum and found the dominant component. The overwhelming majority of the vibration sat at 1P; the 3P component was at a normal level. That single measurement turned the case around completely: the problem was not aerodynamic or tower interaction, it was rotor imbalance. ¶ Separated mass imbalance from aerodynamic imbalance. This distinction matters. Mass imbalance (water collected inside a blade, repair material) acts identically every revolution and is relatively independent of wind speed. Aerodynamic imbalance depends on the wind, because the force comes from the wind. Since the vibration tracked the wind closely, I moved toward aerodynamic imbalance. ¶ Compared the pitch angles of the three blades in SCADA. The control system showed all three at the same angle. On paper there was no problem. But that value came from the encoder — it was the system's belief about where the blade was, not where the blade actually was. ¶ Measured the pitch angle physically. After the turbine was stopped and secured, I went into the hub and measured each of the three blade angles against a reference surface, one by one. Result: one blade sat roughly 2.5° away from th"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/blade-vibration/","b":"Blade Vibration — 1P/3P Harmonics and Pitch Asymmetry","h":"Step-by-step diagnosis","t":"e other two. The encoder was showing all three as identical. ¶ Checked blade–tower clearance. To see whether the vibration was bringing a blade dangerously close to the tower. Clearance was within acceptable limits — there was no immediate risk and the work could be planned rather than rushed. ¶ Inspected the blade roots and pitch bearings. To confirm there was no structural problem. No cracking, delamination or bearing play was found. The blades were sound."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/blade-vibration/","b":"Blade Vibration — 1P/3P Harmonics and Pitch Asymmetry","h":"Root cause","t":"Calibration drift in one blade's pitch encoder. Because the control system believed that blade was at the same angle as the others, it was giving all three the same command; in reality that blade was operating at an angle roughly 2.5° different. ¶ The consequence is that the blade produces a different force from the wind than its neighbours. The rotor is loaded asymmetrically once per revolution — forcing at exactly 1P. In the 7–9 m/s band the rotor speed coincided with the point where that forcing approached a natural frequency of the structure, and the vibration grew there. Outside the band the same asymmetry existed, but without resonance it was not noticeable. ¶ The drop in the power curve came from the same cause: when one blade works outside the optimum angle, the total efficiency of the rotor falls."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/blade-vibration/","b":"Blade Vibration — 1P/3P Harmonics and Pitch Asymmetry","h":"Fix","t":"Pitch encoder calibration (4 hours): all three blades were brought to the physical reference position, encoder zero points redefined, and the values read by the control system matched to the physical measurement. ¶ Verification measurement (1 hour): after calibration the three blades were driven to several different angles and compared against physical measurement at each. The deviation came within tolerance. ¶ Restart and vibration monitoring (2 weeks): the turbine was restarted and vibration re-measured, particularly in the 7–9 m/s band. The 1P component dropped markedly; the perceptible vibration disappeared. ¶ Power curve check (4 weeks): production in that band returned to the expected level."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/blade-vibration/","b":"Blade Vibration — 1P/3P Harmonics and Pitch Asymmetry","h":"Reading a vibration spectrum","t":"Dominant component | What it tells you | Where to look first | ¶ 1P (rotor speed) | Rotor imbalance — one blade is behaving differently | Pitch angle asymmetry, blade mass balance | ¶ 3P (speed × blade count) | Blades passing the tower | Tower shadow, wind profile, yaw alignment | ¶ High frequency, narrow peak | Local damage in a rotating element | Bearing, gearbox, generator | ¶ Broadband rise | Structural loosening | Bolt torques, tower flange, mounts | ¶ Growth in a specific wind band | Resonance — but there is a forcing source | Find the forcing first; resonance only amplifies |"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/blade-vibration/","b":"Blade Vibration — 1P/3P Harmonics and Pitch Asymmetry","h":"Preventive maintenance","t":"Measure pitch angle physically at intervals. Trusting the encoder value is not enough; calibration drifts over time. Comparing against a physical measurement catches this kind of problem before it surfaces. ¶ Always record vibration together with rotor speed. A vibration measurement without the speed cannot tell you whether it is 1P or 3P — that is, it has lost its most valuable information. ¶ Ask for the wind band when a complaint comes in. There is a gulf, diagnostically, between \"there is vibration\" and \"there is vibration between 7 and 9 m/s\". The narrowness of the band points directly at resonance. ¶ Look at the spectrum before deciding on a blade inspection. Blade inspection is expensive and slow. If 1P dominates, the problem is most likely not in the blade but in the blade's angle. ¶ Assess power curve deviation together with vibration. If both degrade in the same band, look for a common cause; in this case the common cause was a single calibration error. ¶ Field note ¶ I like this case because we came very close to making the most expensive mistake on site. The instinct \"there is vibration, so the blade is damaged\" is strong, and it leads straight to a blade inspection or even a blade removal. The fix was a four-hour calibration. ¶ The rule that settled in me from it: do not measure what the encoder shows, measure where the blade actually is. A control system never lies"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/blade-vibration/","b":"Blade Vibration — 1P/3P Harmonics and Pitch Asymmetry","h":"Preventive maintenance","t":"; it simply believes what it has been told. When the calibration drifts the system looks perfectly healthy, because by its own measure everything is in order. A physical measurement is the only thing that closes that blind spot. ¶ Soner SoyluWind Turbine Field Service Technician — Enercon, Nordex, Vestas · GWO certified ¶ Warning: all work in the hub is done with the rotor locked, the turbine stopped and LOTO applied. When working on the pitch system the backup supply must also be isolated. This note is field experience and does not replace the manufacturer's service documentation. ¶ Related ¶ Pitch System — motor, gearbox, sensor and control ¶ Case 01: Pitch Motor High Current ¶ Vestas V126 — model guide ¶ Vestas V136 — vibration management on long blades ¶ All field notes ¶ Fault diagnosis tree (Turkish)"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/converter-overcurrent/","b":"Converter Overcurrent — Only Trips at High Power","h":"","t":"From the field · Case 05 ¶ Converter Overcurrent — The Fault That Only Trips at High Power ¶ A Vestas V110. The turbine runs for days without trouble, then throws a converter fault and stops; you reset it and it runs again. When we went out to service it we could not find anything. These \"disappearing\" faults are the most maddening kind — and almost always they are tied to a condition. Here the condition was power."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/converter-overcurrent/","b":"Converter Overcurrent — Only Trips at High Power","h":"Symptom","t":"Fault code: rotor-side overcurrent / converter fault, followed by automatic shutdown ¶ Frequency: once or twice a month at first, rising to three or four times a week over six weeks ¶ After reset: the turbine comes back online normally and produces for hours without issue ¶ On service: every measurement on the stopped machine reads normal — insulation, phase balance, cable connections, coolant level ¶ Grid side: no disturbance in the voltage or frequency records at the moments of the trips"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/converter-overcurrent/","b":"Converter Overcurrent — Only Trips at High Power","h":"First assessment","t":"Everything reading normal on a stopped turbine is itself information: the fault appears somewhere inside the operating condition and vanishes when the machine stops. That narrows the list to three — a contact problem that shows up with heat, a power-electronics component that only misbehaves under load, or a short grid disturbance too brief to be recorded. ¶ The grid records were clean, so I pushed the third option back. What the remaining two have in common is that both get worse as load rises. So instead of hunting for the faulty part, I first had to find the condition under which the fault appeared."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/converter-overcurrent/","b":"Converter Overcurrent — Only Trips at High Power","h":"Step-by-step diagnosis","t":"Pulled the fault log in raw form. Not just the code — the timestamps. The OEM interface only showed the last 20 entries, so I exported the full list. Nineteen events across six weeks. ¶ Matched every event against the production data at that moment. This is the step that solved the case. I pulled the power values at each trip from SCADA: 17 of the 19 events happened above 2.4 MW. The other two were around 2.3 MW. Not a single event at low power. The fault was not random; it had a threshold. ¶ Confirmed it tracked power, not wind speed. This distinction matters. During hours when the wind was high but the turbine was curtailed, there were no faults. So the trigger was not wind — it was current. ¶ Thermal-imaged the converter cabinet under load. Stopping the turbine to look was useless here; once it cooled there was no trace. With the turbine producing, from a safe distance, I took readings inside the cabinet. One of the DC busbar joints came out roughly 30 °C hotter than its neighbours. ¶ Checked the IGBT modules separately. In the same scan one module was also running hotter than the others, but by a smaller margin (8–10 °C). I noted it without declaring it guilty on its own. ¶ Stopped the turbine and torque-checked that joint. The bolt on the hot busbar was well below the OEM value. A loose power connection raises contact resistance; resistance generates heat with the square o"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/converter-overcurrent/","b":"Converter Overcurrent — Only Trips at High Power","h":"Step-by-step diagnosis","t":"f the current. Negligible at low power, decisive at high power. That was the threshold. ¶ Opened and inspected the contact face. There was oxidation and slight discolouration — the mark of something that has been heating and cooling for a long time."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/converter-overcurrent/","b":"Converter Overcurrent — Only Trips at High Power","h":"Root cause","t":"A loosened DC busbar joint. Contact resistance had risen, the joint overheated at high current, and that heat was pulling the working temperature of the neighbouring IGBT module up with it. Running hot, the module reached its own protection threshold earlier and tripped as an overcurrent. ¶ So two things were true at once: the joint was the real cause, but the module had genuinely been stressed. Simply tightening the bolt and leaving would have left an already fatigued module in place. ¶ Chain of evidence: the fault only appears above 2.3 MW → a heat problem that emerges at high current → a 30 °C hot spot under load on thermal imaging → torque below the OEM value at that joint → oxidation on the contact face. Each step confirms the one before it."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/converter-overcurrent/","b":"Converter Overcurrent — Only Trips at High Power","h":"Fix","t":"Joint refurbishment (2 hours): busbar removed, contact faces cleaned of oxide, reassembled with proper contact compound and torqued to the OEM value in a star pattern. ¶ Check of every power connection in the cabinet (3 hours): if one has loosened, the others are suspect. All were torque-checked; two more were marginal and corrected. ¶ Replacement of the stressed IGBT module (planned, 1 day): no spare on site that day. The turbine was temporarily run with a 2.2 MW power cap — below the threshold, so the fault did not recur and the machine stayed in production. The module was swapped when it arrived. ¶ Follow-up (4 weeks): with the cap lifted, the fault log was watched for four weeks. No new events."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/converter-overcurrent/","b":"Converter Overcurrent — Only Trips at High Power","h":"How the fault related to the power threshold","t":"Production band | Time spent in band | Faults (6 weeks) | Comment | ¶ 0 – 1.5 MW | Most of the time | 0 | Current low, contact resistance irrelevant | ¶ 1.5 – 2.3 MW | Moderate | 0 | Heating present but below threshold | ¶ 2.3 – 2.4 MW | Little | 2 | Start of the threshold | ¶ 2.4 MW and above | Least | 17 | Almost every fault sits here | ¶ The real message in the table: the fault clustered in the band where the turbine spent the least time. That is why it looked random. Matching events to power rather than to elapsed time is what made the pattern appear."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/converter-overcurrent/","b":"Converter Overcurrent — Only Trips at High Power","h":"Preventive maintenance","t":"Periodic torque checks on power connections: converter and transformer joints should be torque-checked at the OEM interval. It is one of the easiest jobs to skip and one of the highest-return ones. ¶ Thermal scanning under load: an annual thermal survey taken while the turbine is producing catches problems that are invisible on a stopped machine. A scan of a stopped machine would never have found this fault. ¶ Read the fault log together with power: the code alone says little. Put every event next to the power, wind and temperature at that moment. ¶ Record reset counts on intermittent faults: a rising frequency is the earliest sign that the problem is progressing. Going from monthly to three times a week was our signal to stop waiting. ¶ Field note ¶ What I took from this case is a method, not a technical fact. With intermittent faults you look for the condition rather than the part. \"When does it happen?\" comes before \"what broke?\". Here the condition was power; in other cases it has been temperature, wind direction or time of day. ¶ A second note: a temporary power cap is a very useful tool in this work. Rather than stopping the turbine completely while waiting for a part, we ran it below the threshold — production continued and our diagnosis was proved in practice. When the fault did not recur, we knew the threshold hypothesis was right. ¶ Soner SoyluWind Turbine Field Servi"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/converter-overcurrent/","b":"Converter Overcurrent — Only Trips at High Power","h":"Preventive maintenance","t":"ce Technician — Enercon, Nordex, Vestas · GWO certified ¶ Warning: a converter cabinet carries high voltage, and dangerous energy remains in the DC-link capacitors even after shutdown. No work is done inside the cabinet before the OEM's stated discharge time has elapsed and the absence of voltage has been verified by measurement. This note is field experience and does not replace service documentation. ¶ Related ¶ Generator and Converter System — DFIG and power electronics ¶ Vestas V110 — model guide ¶ Case 04: Generator Temperature Alarm ¶ All field notes ¶ Fault diagnosis tree (Turkish)"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/gearbox-temperature/","b":"Gearbox Temperature Rising With Wind Speed","h":"","t":"From the field · Case 02 ¶ Gearbox Temperature Rising With Wind Speed ¶ A Vestas V126. The gearbox oil was running at 78–82 °C where it used to sit at 60–65 °C. Still below the alarm threshold, so nothing had stopped — and that is precisely why it was worth looking at. A temperature that has moved without tripping anything is a machine telling you something quietly."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/gearbox-temperature/","b":"Gearbox Temperature Rising With Wind Speed","h":"Symptom","t":"Oil temperature: 78–82 °C, against a normal band of 60–65 °C for the same conditions ¶ Alarm: none yet; the threshold is 80 °C with a short averaging window, so it was only brushing it ¶ Pattern: the gap widened as wind speed rose — at low wind the temperature was almost normal ¶ Oil: level below the mark, colour dark, a burnt smell, metallic particles present"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/gearbox-temperature/","b":"Gearbox Temperature Rising With Wind Speed","h":"First assessment","t":"The detail that mattered was that the rise tracked wind speed. A blocked radiator or a failed cooling fan raises temperature fairly uniformly. A rise that grows with load points at the oil itself: either there is not enough of it, or it has lost its ability to do the job. ¶ The state of the oil settled it quickly — low level, dark colour, burnt smell. But a low level is a symptom, not a cause. Oil does not evaporate. The real question was where it was going."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/gearbox-temperature/","b":"Gearbox Temperature Rising With Wind Speed","h":"Step-by-step diagnosis","t":"Read temperature against wind speed rather than against time. Plotting the two together made the relationship obvious and ruled out a cooling-side fault, which would not have scaled with load this way. ¶ Checked the cooling circuit anyway. Radiator clean, fan running, coolant at level. Eliminating the obvious alternative is worth the twenty minutes. ¶ Sampled and inspected the oil. Dark, burnt smell, metallic particles. The oil had been running hot long enough to begin oxidising, and wear had started. ¶ Followed the oil trace to find the loss. There was oil on the housing below the high-speed shaft. The seal there had hardened and was passing oil. ¶ Estimated the quantity lost. Against the fill volume and the level drop, something over 50 litres had gone — consistent with a slow leak rather than a sudden failure. ¶ Pulled the eight-month trend. The pitch of the curve told the story: temperature had crept up month by month, and in parallel the drive motor current had risen from about 11 A to 18 A. Two independent numbers describing the same decline."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/gearbox-temperature/","b":"Gearbox Temperature Rising With Wind Speed","h":"Root cause","t":"A degraded shaft seal had been leaking oil for months. As the level fell, the remaining oil had to carry more heat, its temperature rose, and above roughly 75 °C viscosity drops and oxidation accelerates — which degrades the oil further and raises the temperature again. A self-reinforcing loop, running slowly enough that no alarm ever fired."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/gearbox-temperature/","b":"Gearbox Temperature Rising With Wind Speed","h":"Temperature against wind speed — reference","t":"Wind speed | Expected oil temperature | What we measured | Gap | ¶ 4–6 m/s | 45–52 °C | 52–56 °C | small | ¶ 7–9 m/s | 55–62 °C | 66–71 °C | growing | ¶ 10–12 m/s | 60–65 °C | 78–82 °C | large | ¶ 13+ m/s | 63–68 °C | brushing the threshold | largest | ¶ The shape of this table is the diagnosis. A fault that grows with load is an oil problem; a fault that is flat across load is a cooling problem."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/gearbox-temperature/","b":"Gearbox Temperature Rising With Wind Speed","h":"Fix","t":"Seal replacement (4 hours): the failed shaft seal was replaced and the sealing face inspected for scoring. ¶ Full oil change (3 hours): the entire charge was drained rather than topped up — the old oil was already oxidised and carrying wear metal. ¶ Filter replacement: done at the same time, since the existing filter had been loaded by the contaminated oil. ¶ Follow-up sampling (3 and 6 months): metal concentration was tracked to confirm the wear had stopped progressing."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/gearbox-temperature/","b":"Gearbox Temperature Rising With Wind Speed","h":"Preventive maintenance","t":"Record oil temperature together with wind speed. A temperature without a load reference cannot be interpreted. The same 70 °C is normal at 12 m/s and alarming at 5 m/s. ¶ Follow the trend at a fixed wind speed. Pick a band, say 9–10 m/s, and plot the temperature in that band month to month. The slope is the early warning. ¶ Treat a falling oil level as a leak until proven otherwise. Topping up without finding the path only buys time and hides the trend. ¶ Take oil samples seriously. Colour, smell and metal content together tell you more than the level gauge. ¶ Watch drive current alongside temperature. Two unrelated instruments moving in the same direction is strong confirmation. ¶ Field note ¶ This case is the clearest example I have of why a threshold is not a diagnosis. The turbine never crossed the alarm limit. Everything it needed to tell us was in the gap between the temperature and the wind speed — and that gap is only visible if you plot the two together. ¶ The other lesson is about topping up. Adding oil makes the symptom disappear for a few weeks and destroys the trend that would have found the leak. If the level is down, find where it went before you fill it. ¶ Soner SoyluWind Turbine Field Service Technician — Enercon, Nordex, Vestas · GWO certified ¶ Warning: Gearbox oil runs hot and the housing stays hot long after shutdown; the turbine must be stopped, the rotor "},{"u":"/en/field-notes/gearbox-temperature/","b":"Gearbox Temperature Rising With Wind Speed","h":"Preventive maintenance","t":"locked and LOTO applied before any work. This note is field experience and does not replace the manufacturer's service documentation. ¶ Related ¶ Gearbox System — parallel-axis and planetary designs ¶ Vestas V126 — model guide ¶ Case 01: Pitch Motor High Current ¶ All field notes ¶ Fault diagnosis tree (Turkish)"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/generator-temperature/","b":"Generator Temperature Alarm — Is the Fault in the Sensor?","h":"","t":"From the field · Case 04 ¶ Generator Temperature Alarm — Is the Fault in the Sensor? ¶ Vestas V126. When the generator winding temperature reaches 116 °C the turbine stops on the alarm, comes back after a reset, and stops again the same day. Yet the rest of the machine looks healthy: normal sound, no vibration, cooling running. When the reading and the machine's behaviour disagree, one of them is wrong — and it is worth finding out which before you touch the generator."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/generator-temperature/","b":"Generator Temperature Alarm — Is the Fault in the Sensor?","h":"Symptom","t":"Alarm: Generator temperature high, 116 °C (threshold 115 °C). The turbine stops. ¶ After a reset: the turbine comes back normally; the alarm repeats 2–3 times a day in the same wind. ¶ Timing: the alarms come in the afternoon, at rated power (13–15 m/s wind). ¶ Trend: over the last three days the peak reading went 110 → 112 → 116 °C — a tidy climb of about 2 °C per day. ¶ Everything else: power, current and speed normal. No unusual sound, vibration or smell."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/generator-temperature/","b":"Generator Temperature Alarm — Is the Fault in the Sensor?","h":"First assessment","t":"A generator genuinely overheating does not stay quiet. Its sound changes, it smells, and the cooling circuit shows the load somewhere. None of that was present. A severe number with none of its consequences is the classic shape of an instrumentation fault rather than a machine fault. ¶ The other clue was the trend. A real thermal problem tracks load and weather; it does not climb a tidy 2 °C every day regardless. A steady, load-independent drift points to something moving in the measurement chain, not to the machine heating up."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/generator-temperature/","b":"Generator Temperature Alarm — Is the Fault in the Sensor?","h":"Step-by-step diagnosis","t":"Reviewed the SCADA log together with the load data. At the alarm moments power, current and speed were at rated values; the generator was not overloaded. The temperature rose day by day, not with load. ¶ Stopped the turbine and took a physical reading with an IR thermometer. This is the step that decides the case. The stopped generator's housing measured about 62 °C while SCADA showed 103 °C at the same moment — a gap of 41 °C. A sensor inside the winding reads somewhat hotter than the housing surface; a few degrees is normal, 41 is not. ¶ Took several readings around the housing. To be sure the IR reading was not a local artefact. The distribution was even; there was no local hot spot. ¶ Checked the cooling circuit. Fan running (3.2 A), airflow normal, filter clean. Nothing to explain a real 116 °C, which reinforced the instrumentation hypothesis. ¶ Measured the sensor resistance. A PT100 is 100 Ω at 0 °C and rises by roughly 0.385 Ω per degree. For the measured 62 °C the expected value is about 124 Ω. The sensor read about 140 Ω, which corresponds to roughly 103 °C in the PT100 table. SCADA was converting the resistance correctly; the resistance itself was wrong. ¶ Checked the wiring and terminals. A loose or corroded terminal adds series resistance and produces exactly this picture. The connections were sound, so the deviation was in the sensor element itself. ¶ Reviewed the"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/generator-temperature/","b":"Generator Temperature Alarm — Is the Fault in the Sensor?","h":"Step-by-step diagnosis","t":" sensor's service age. It had been in service for about four years. ¶ Chain of evidence: a load-independent, steadily climbing temperature → a 41 °C gap between IR and SCADA → nothing wrong with cooling → the PT100 resistance matches the SCADA temperature, not the real one → connections sound. Each step confirms the one before."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/generator-temperature/","b":"Generator Temperature Alarm — Is the Fault in the Sensor?","h":"Root cause","t":"Drift in the winding temperature sensor (PT100). The element showed about 16 Ω too much resistance, which lifted the reading by roughly 41 °C. SCADA faithfully converted that wrong resistance into a temperature, and the control system did exactly what it was designed to do — protect the machine — by stopping a generator that was in good health. ¶ The cost of this fault was not damage. It was lost production, plus the risk of someone opening up a healthy generator chasing a number."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/generator-temperature/","b":"Generator Temperature Alarm — Is the Fault in the Sensor?","h":"Fix","t":"Temporary correction: a correction based on the physically verified gap was entered in SCADA and the turbine returned to production. This is done under the site's and the manufacturer's procedure, with authorisation, documented and with an end date. While it is in place the reading is compared with an IR measurement regularly; if the drift continues, the gap changes. If the generator has a spare winding sensor, switching to it is safer than entering a correction. ¶ Alarm threshold left unchanged: a correction and a raised threshold together would hide a real overheat twice over. The threshold is not changed while the cause is unknown or while a correction is in place. ¶ Sensor replacement planned: once the new sensor is fitted, its reading is verified against the IR thermometer at several load points. ¶ Temporary correction removed: when the new sensor is confirmed accurate, the correction comes out. That step is part of the repair — a forgotten correction will one day hide a real fault."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/generator-temperature/","b":"Generator Temperature Alarm — Is the Fault in the Sensor?","h":"Real overheating or sensor drift?","t":"Check | Real overheating | Sensor drift | ¶ IR thermometer vs SCADA | Agree within a few degrees | Large, consistent gap | ¶ Relationship to load | Rises and falls with load | Largely independent of load | ¶ Trend shape | Irregular, weather-driven | Steady day-by-day climb | ¶ Sound and smell | Usually changed | Normal | ¶ Cooling circuit | Often shows a cause | Nothing to find | ¶ PT100 resistance | Matches the measured temperature | Does not match |"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/generator-temperature/","b":"Generator Temperature Alarm — Is the Fault in the Sensor?","h":"PT100 resistance table","t":"Temperature | 0 °C | 20 °C | 40 °C | 60 °C | 80 °C | 100 °C | 120 °C | ¶ Resistance | 100.0 Ω | 107.8 Ω | 115.5 Ω | 123.2 Ω | 130.9 Ω | 138.5 Ω | 146.1 Ω | ¶ Standard IEC 60751 PT100 values. With a two-wire connection the cable resistance adds to the reading; measure at the terminal closest to the sensor where possible and note the cable resistance separately."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/generator-temperature/","b":"Generator Temperature Alarm — Is the Fault in the Sensor?","h":"Preventive maintenance","t":"On any temperature alarm, take a physical reading first. An IR thermometer costs minutes and settles whether you have a machine problem or a measurement problem. ¶ Treat RTD sensors as consumables. They can drift with age. Add a resistance check to the periodic schedule rather than waiting for a nonsense reading. ¶ Log temporary corrections and put an end date on them. A correction that becomes permanent will one day hide a real fault. ¶ Read the trend shape, not just the value. A tidy, load-independent daily climb is almost never a real thermal problem."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/generator-temperature/","b":"Generator Temperature Alarm — Is the Fault in the Sensor?","h":"Quick checklist","t":"Measure the generator housing with an IR thermometer and read SCADA at the same moment. Is the gap larger than 10 °C? ¶ Were power, current and speed normal at the alarm moments? ¶ Is the cooling fan running, is its current normal, is the filter clean? ¶ Measure the PT100 resistance and convert it with the table. Does it match the temperature you measured? ¶ Are the terminal and cable connections tight and clean? ¶ What do the other winding sensors in the same generator show? ¶ Does the trend follow load, or climb steadily day by day? ¶ Field note ¶ The rule I carry out of this one: when a number and the machine's behaviour disagree, believe the machine until you have proved the number. A generator genuinely at 116 °C announces itself. This one was quiet, and the quiet was the evidence. ¶ The part that needs discipline is the temporary correction. It is the right move to keep the turbine producing while you wait for a sensor — and exactly the kind of fix that becomes permanent if nobody writes it down. Removing it after the replacement is part of the repair, not an afterthought. ¶ Soner SoyluWind Turbine Field Service Technician — Enercon, Nordex, Vestas · GWO certified ¶ Warning: the generator terminal box carries high voltage. Sensor measurement and replacement are done only with the turbine stopped, the circuit isolated, absence of voltage verified and LOTO applied. Correcti"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/generator-temperature/","b":"Generator Temperature Alarm — Is the Fault in the Sensor?","h":"Quick checklist","t":"ons or parameter changes in SCADA are made only with authorisation and under the manufacturer's procedure. This note is field experience and does not replace the manufacturer's service documentation. ¶ Related ¶ Solve this case in the simulator ¶ Generator and Converter System ¶ Case 05: Converter Overcurrent ¶ Vestas V126 — model guide ¶ All field notes ¶ Fault diagnosis tree (Turkish)"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/","b":"Field Fault Cases — Step-by-Step Diagnosis Notes","h":"","t":"From the field ¶ Field Notes ¶ These are not from a book; they came out of the tower. Each one follows a single fault from start to finish: what the alarm said, what I thought it was, which measurement I took, which possibilities it ruled out, what the root cause turned out to be and how we closed it. The point is not the answer — it is the order of the diagnosis. ¶ A fault tree gives you a fast route from symptom to cause; these pages show you what it is like inside one case. The two complement each other: narrow it down with the tree first, then read the closest case here. ¶ Note: these are field experience, not a substitute for the manufacturer's service documentation. Your own turbine's OEM instructions, LOTO procedure and site safety rules govern every intervention. ¶ Case 01Pitch Motor High CurrentPitch motor drawing 18 A instead of 12 A on a Nordex N117. Six-step diagnosis; root cause was a gearbox oil leak. Case 02Gearbox Temperature RiseOil temperature at 78–82 °C on a Vestas V126. Eight-month trend analysis; oil loss through a failing shaft seal. Case 03Yaw HuntingNacelle oscillating ±18° on an Enercon E92. Bearing drag, sensor jitter and a drifted PLC parameter. Case 04Generator Temperature AlarmA 116 °C alarm on a Vestas V126. A 41 °C gap between the IR thermometer and SCADA: sensor calibration drift. Case 05Converter OvercurrentAn intermittent fault on a Vestas V11"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/","b":"Field Fault Cases — Step-by-Step Diagnosis Notes","h":"","t":"0 that only tripped above 2.3 MW. A loosened DC busbar joint, found by chasing the condition. Case 06Yaw Bearing WearDamage caught with no alarm on an Enercon E92. A blocked lubrication line starved one sector of the race. Case 07Blade VibrationVibration in a narrow 7–9 m/s band on a Vestas V126. 1P harmonic, 2.5° pitch asymmetry — calibration, not the blade."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/","b":"Field Fault Cases — Step-by-Step Diagnosis Notes","h":"What should you do first when a turbine faults?","t":"Before you read the alarm, make it safe: stop the turbine, apply LOTO, isolate the system you are going to touch. Then pull the OEM fault log and the SCADA trend. The trend tells you far more than a single instantaneous reading: did this start today, or has it been growing for weeks?"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/","b":"Field Fault Cases — Step-by-Step Diagnosis Notes","h":"Why does a SCADA value differ from what you measure on site?","t":"The most common reason is sensor calibration drift. RTD temperature sensors in particular change resistance over the years and SCADA then reports a value that does not exist. That is why the first move on a temperature alarm is to take a physical reading with an IR thermometer and compare: a large gap tells you the fault is in the sensor."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/pitch-motor-high-current/","b":"Pitch Motor High Current — Field Diagnosis and Fix","h":"","t":"From the field · Case 01 ¶ Pitch Motor High Current — Field Diagnosis and Fix ¶ A Nordex N117. The pitch motor on one blade was pulling around 18 A where the other two sat at 12 A. Nothing had failed yet and the turbine was still producing — which is exactly the kind of fault worth catching, because the machine is telling you something before it breaks."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/pitch-motor-high-current/","b":"Pitch Motor High Current — Field Diagnosis and Fix","h":"Symptom","t":"SCADA: pitch motor current on blade 2 at 17–18 A; blades 1 and 3 at 11–12 A under the same conditions ¶ Behaviour: pitch movement completes, but more slowly than the other two blades ¶ Alarm: intermittent \"pitch motor current high\" warning, no shutdown yet ¶ Trend: looking back through SCADA, the current had been climbing gradually for about two months"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/pitch-motor-high-current/","b":"Pitch Motor High Current — Field Diagnosis and Fix","h":"First assessment","t":"A motor pulling more current is doing more work. On a pitch axis that means one of three things: the electrical side has a problem (winding, connection, drive), something mechanical is resisting the movement, or the blade itself has become harder to turn. The fact that the rise was gradual pushed me away from an electrical fault — electrical faults tend to appear suddenly. A slow climb over two months is the signature of something mechanical getting worse."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/pitch-motor-high-current/","b":"Pitch Motor High Current — Field Diagnosis and Fix","h":"Step-by-step diagnosis","t":"Turned the blade by hand at the gearbox output. With the turbine stopped, rotor locked and the pitch drive isolated. The resistance on blade 2 was noticeably higher than on the other two. That single check moved the fault from \"electrical\" to \"mechanical\" and saved a lot of time. ¶ Measured motor winding resistance. To rule out the motor itself. All three phases were balanced and matched the other two motors. The motor was healthy; it was simply working against something. ¶ Checked the pitch gearbox oil. This is where it was found. The level was below the mark, the oil was dark, and there was a metallic sheen in it. The gearbox was running short of lubrication. ¶ Looked for the leak path. Oil does not disappear. There was a trace of oil on the gearbox housing and, following it up, a hairline crack roughly 2 mm long on the casing near the mounting face. ¶ Checked the sensors. The pitch encoder and limit switches read correctly and matched the physical position, so the control side was not commanding anything unusual. ¶ Went back to the SCADA trend. Comparing the current climb against the estimated oil loss lined up: as the level fell, the current rose. The story was consistent from both ends."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/pitch-motor-high-current/","b":"Pitch Motor High Current — Field Diagnosis and Fix","h":"Root cause","t":"A hairline crack in the pitch gearbox casing had been leaking oil slowly over roughly two to three months. As the level dropped, the gear set ran with progressively poorer lubrication, friction rose, and the motor compensated by drawing more current. The metallic sheen in the oil showed wear had already begun inside. ¶ Nothing here happened suddenly. The turbine had been signalling for two months through a number in SCADA that nobody had plotted as a trend."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/pitch-motor-high-current/","b":"Pitch Motor High Current — Field Diagnosis and Fix","h":"Fix","t":"Containment (1 hour): the leak was temporarily sealed and the oil topped up so the machine could be brought to a safe state without running the gears dry. ¶ Permanent repair (3 hours): the crack was TIG welded and the repair checked by non-destructive testing before the gearbox was put back into service. ¶ Oil replacement (2 hours): the contaminated oil was drained completely and replaced with fresh oil to the correct level, since the old charge already carried wear metal. ¶ Total downtime: about six hours. Caught later, this would have been a gearbox replacement."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/pitch-motor-high-current/","b":"Pitch Motor High Current — Field Diagnosis and Fix","h":"Preventive maintenance","t":"Compare the three pitch motor currents against each other, not against a threshold. An 18 A reading may sit below the alarm limit and still be wrong if its two siblings are at 12 A. The asymmetry is the signal. ¶ Plot the trend, not the value. A single reading says almost nothing. The same number over eight weeks says everything. ¶ Include the pitch gearbox in visual inspections. An oil trace on the casing is the cheapest early warning you will ever get. ¶ Turn the blade by hand when you suspect a pitch fault. It takes two minutes and separates mechanical from electrical faster than any instrument. ¶ Field note ¶ The habit that pays here is boring: look at the three blades side by side. A pitch fault almost never starts as a failure — it starts as one axis behaving slightly differently from the other two. If you read each value against the alarm threshold instead of against its siblings, you will miss it every time. ¶ The second thing is that a six-hour repair and a gearbox replacement were separated only by the two months of trend data nobody had looked at. ¶ Soner SoyluWind Turbine Field Service Technician — Enercon, Nordex, Vestas · GWO certified ¶ Warning: All work in the hub is done with the rotor locked, the turbine stopped, the pitch drive and its backup supply isolated, and LOTO applied. This note is field experience and does not replace the manufacturer's service docum"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/pitch-motor-high-current/","b":"Pitch Motor High Current — Field Diagnosis and Fix","h":"Preventive maintenance","t":"entation. ¶ Related ¶ Pitch System — motor, gearbox, sensor and control ¶ Turbine model guide ¶ Case 07: Blade Vibration — the same system, a different fault ¶ All field notes ¶ Fault diagnosis tree (Turkish)"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/yaw-bearing-wear/","b":"Yaw Bearing Wear — The Metal in the Purged Grease","h":"","t":"From the field · Case 06 ¶ Yaw Bearing Wear — The Metal in the Purged Grease ¶ What makes this case different from the others is that there was no alarm. The turbine was running, SCADA was clean, nobody had complained. We caught the fault during a routine greasing job, while looking at the colour of the old grease coming out. Enercon E92, scheduled annual service."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/yaw-bearing-wear/","b":"Yaw Bearing Wear — The Metal in the Purged Grease","h":"Symptom","t":"Alarm: none. The turbine was producing normally. ¶ First sign: some of the old grease purging out during lubrication was dark grey with a metallic glitter. Grease from the other points looked normal. ¶ Yaw motor current: average value within normal limits in SCADA, but peak values had crept up over recent months. ¶ Sound: an audible friction noise as the yaw passed through one particular position."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/yaw-bearing-wear/","b":"Yaw Bearing Wear — The Metal in the Purged Grease","h":"First assessment","t":"Metal in grease is not on its own a reason to panic; a new bearing sheds some metal during run-in. What stood out here was not that metal was present but how it was distributed. If every greasing point on the slewing ring had produced metal, that would be general ageing. Metal from only some points said the damage was in a specific region of the bearing, not across it. ¶ There are two classic reasons for damage to concentrate in one region of a slewing bearing: either that region is carrying disproportionate load, or that region is not being lubricated. The second is far more common, and the reason is almost always the same — a blocked line feeding it."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/yaw-bearing-wear/","b":"Yaw Bearing Wear — The Metal in the Purged Grease","h":"Step-by-step diagnosis","t":"Collected the purged grease separately, point by point. Putting it all in one container would have killed the case. I collected the grease from each greasing point separately, labelled with its number. That became the basis of every step that followed. ¶ Spread each sample thinly on a white surface. Simple but effective: spread as a thin film, metal particles in the grease become visible to the naked eye. I also checked with a magnet — the particles being magnetic supported bearing steel as the source. ¶ Drew a sector map. I marked how much metal came from which point around the circumference of the bearing. The pattern was unmistakable: metal was concentrated at three adjacent greasing points and effectively absent at the rest. The damage sat in roughly a quarter of the ring. ¶ Checked the automatic lubrication system. The pump was running and the reservoir was full — no problem at first glance. But following each line out of the distributor, I found a hardened grease plug at the connection feeding the sector that was shedding metal. ¶ Confirmed it by opening the line. With the pump running, grease came out of the other lines and not out of the blocked one. The diagnosis was settled at this step: that sector had not been fed for some time. ¶ Measured ring gear backlash. To see whether the wear had spread from the bearing into the gear. Backlash was within the manufacturer's to"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/yaw-bearing-wear/","b":"Yaw Bearing Wear — The Metal in the Purged Grease","h":"Step-by-step diagnosis","t":"lerance — good news; the damage had not reached the gear. ¶ Located the noise by watching the yaw move. The friction noise occurred exactly as the nacelle passed through the sector that was shedding metal. Three independent pieces of evidence — grease, lubrication line, noise — all pointed at the same region."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/yaw-bearing-wear/","b":"Yaw Bearing Wear — The Metal in the Purged Grease","h":"Root cause","t":"A blockage in the automatic lubrication line had left one sector of the slewing bearing without grease. That region had started running dry, and wear and light scoring had formed on the raceway. The metal particles it shed then became a second factor accelerating the wear — it had entered a self-feeding loop. ¶ How long it took to develop cannot be said precisely, but the hardness of the old grease and the level of wear suggested a process measured in months. The reason it produced no alarm is simple: the yaw motor can absorb rising friction for a long time by drawing more current, and that rise never reaches the alarm threshold."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/yaw-bearing-wear/","b":"Yaw Bearing Wear — The Metal in the Purged Grease","h":"Fix","t":"Clearing the lubrication line (2 hours): the blocked line was removed, cleaned and its fitting replaced. With the pump running, grease delivery was then verified line by line. ¶ Heavy greasing of the bearing (3 hours): the starved sector was greased in stages until the old grease and metal particles had been pushed out. Continued until the purged grease came back clean. ¶ Short-interval follow-up (3 months): the normally annual grease check was done at three months for this turbine, to see whether the metal was reducing. ¶ Result: at the second check the metal had clearly reduced; by the third it was at normal levels. The wear had stopped. No bearing replacement was needed."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/yaw-bearing-wear/","b":"Yaw Bearing Wear — The Metal in the Purged Grease","h":"What to look for in purged grease","t":"Appearance | Likely meaning | What to do | ¶ Light colour, even consistency | Normal | Continue at the standard interval | ¶ Darkened but no metal | Ageing, contamination | Review the greasing interval | ¶ Fine metallic glitter, widespread | General ageing or run-in | Record it, compare at the next service | ¶ Metallic glitter in one region only | Local lack of lubrication | Check the lubrication lines | ¶ Coarse particles or chips | Advanced damage | Stop, inspect in detail, consult the OEM | ¶ Milky, water present | Loss of sealing | Check seals and cover gaskets |"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/yaw-bearing-wear/","b":"Yaw Bearing Wear — The Metal in the Purged Grease","h":"Preventive maintenance","t":"Collect purged grease point by point. This whole case rests on that single habit. Put it all in one container and the pattern disappears; you are left with \"there is some metal\". ¶ Keep a sector map. Mark what came from which point around the ring and keep the record. Being able to compare at the next service is worth many times a one-off observation. ¶ Do not accept \"the pump is running\" as lubrication. A running pump does not mean grease is reaching every point. Verify delivery at each line outlet. ¶ Watch the peak values of yaw motor current. While the average still looks normal, a climb in the peaks is the early sign of rising friction. ¶ Record where the noise occurs. \"There is a noise when the yaw turns\" is an inadequate record. Write down the yaw angle it happens at — it takes the diagnosis straight to that region. ¶ Field note ¶ This case reminds me why there is a difference between maintenance \"looking done\" and maintenance \"being done\". The greasing had been carried out, it was ticked in the records, and the pump was running. The only thing missing was looking at the grease that came out. A five-minute job saved a slewing bearing. ¶ I also learned this: waiting for an alarm is an expensive habit. The yaw motor absorbs rising friction quietly for a long time, and by the time the alarm arrives the damage has gone a long way. On this system the early warning does not com"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/yaw-bearing-wear/","b":"Yaw Bearing Wear — The Metal in the Purged Grease","h":"Preventive maintenance","t":"e from SCADA — it comes from the old grease in your hand. ¶ Soner SoyluWind Turbine Field Service Technician — Enercon, Nordex, Vestas · GWO certified ¶ Warning: before any work on the yaw system the turbine must be stopped, the yaw drive isolated and LOTO applied. This note is field experience and does not replace the manufacturer's service documentation. ¶ Related ¶ Yaw System — slewing bearing, ring gear, vane sensor ¶ Case 03: Yaw Hunting — the same system, a different fault ¶ Enercon E92 — model guide ¶ All field notes ¶ Fault diagnosis tree (Turkish)"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/yaw-hunting/","b":"Yaw Hunting — The Nacelle Oscillates While Turning","h":"","t":"From the field · Case 03 ¶ Yaw Hunting — The Nacelle Oscillates While Turning ¶ An Enercon E92. Instead of settling into the wind, the nacelle kept overshooting and coming back — swinging about ±18° at roughly 0.5 to 1 Hz. Yaw hunting is one of those faults where looking for a single culprit wastes your day, because it is usually several small things adding up."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/yaw-hunting/","b":"Yaw Hunting — The Nacelle Oscillates While Turning","h":"Symptom","t":"Behaviour: the nacelle oscillates ±18° about the wind direction instead of settling ¶ Frequency: roughly 0.5–1.0 Hz ¶ Yaw motor current: elevated, because the drive is working almost continuously ¶ Production: below expectation, since the rotor spends much of its time off-axis ¶ Sound: a friction noise from the yaw deck during movement"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/yaw-hunting/","b":"Yaw Hunting — The Nacelle Oscillates While Turning","h":"First assessment","t":"Yaw is a closed loop: a sensor reports the wind direction, a controller decides, a drive moves the nacelle, and the sensor reads the result. Hunting means that loop has become unstable. Instability comes from one of three places — the sensor is feeding in noise, the controller is reacting too hard, or the mechanics are not responding the way the controller expects. ¶ The friction noise made me suspect the mechanical side, but I did not want to assume. With a control loop it pays to examine every element, because the answer is often a combination rather than a single fault."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/yaw-hunting/","b":"Yaw Hunting — The Nacelle Oscillates While Turning","h":"Step-by-step diagnosis","t":"Looked at the SCADA trend for the oscillation shape. A regular, sustained oscillation rather than a decaying one. That already suggests the controller is feeding the motion rather than damping it. ¶ Drove the yaw manually in controlled steps. With the automatic loop disabled. The nacelle moved, but not smoothly — the resistance varied through the rotation. ¶ Checked bearing drag. Measuring the current needed to start and sustain the movement showed it was higher than it should be, and it varied by position. The bearing was dragging. ¶ Examined the vane sensor. Its mounting was slightly loose. A loose sensor produces small, fast fluctuations in the reported direction — jitter — which a controller will faithfully chase. ¶ Checked the drive electronics. Motor windings and drive output were normal. Nothing to explain the behaviour on the electrical side. ¶ Read the controller parameters. The proportional gain sat at 1.5 against a design value of 0.6. A gain that high makes the loop react aggressively to every small error, including sensor noise. This was the piece that turned a sluggish mechanism into an oscillation."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/yaw-hunting/","b":"Yaw Hunting — The Nacelle Oscillates While Turning","h":"Root cause","t":"Three factors compounding. The bearing was dragging, so the nacelle did not respond the way the controller expected. The vane sensor was loose, so the controller was being fed jitter. And the proportional gain had been left at 1.5 instead of 0.6, so the controller amplified both problems instead of absorbing them. ¶ Any one of these alone would have produced a mildly imperfect yaw. Together they produced a loop that would not settle. This is the normal shape of a control-loop fault: no single part is dramatically broken, but the combination is unstable."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/yaw-hunting/","b":"Yaw Hunting — The Nacelle Oscillates While Turning","h":"Fix","t":"Controller parameter restored (30 minutes): proportional gain set back to the design value of 0.6. ¶ Vane sensor remounted (1 hour): mounting tightened and alignment verified, removing the jitter at source. ¶ Bearing lubrication (2 hours): the slewing bearing was lubricated and the lubrication delivery checked at each point. ¶ Result: the oscillation narrowed to about ±2–3° within two weeks, which is normal operating behaviour, and the yaw motor current came back down."},{"u":"/en/field-notes/yaw-hunting/","b":"Yaw Hunting — The Nacelle Oscillates While Turning","h":"Preventive maintenance","t":"Treat hunting as a loop problem, not a part problem. Check the sensor, the controller and the mechanics in turn; expect the answer to be a combination. ¶ Record controller parameters after every intervention. A gain left at a commissioning or troubleshooting value is a common and invisible cause. ¶ Check vane sensor mounting at every service. It takes a minute and a loose sensor poisons every downstream decision. ¶ Watch yaw motor current as a mechanical health signal. Rising current with no change in duty is the earliest sign of bearing drag. ¶ Field note ¶ The temptation with hunting is to keep adjusting the controller until the oscillation stops. You can usually make it go away that way — by detuning the loop so far that the yaw becomes lazy. The mechanical problem is still there, and now it is hidden. ¶ What worked here was fixing the mechanics and the sensor first, then putting the gain back where it was designed to be, rather than compensating for the other two faults with a number. ¶ Soner SoyluWind Turbine Field Service Technician — Enercon, Nordex, Vestas · GWO certified ¶ Warning: Work on the yaw system requires the turbine stopped, the yaw drive isolated and LOTO applied; the yaw deck is a place where pinch injuries happen. This note is field experience and does not replace the manufacturer's service documentation. ¶ Related ¶ Yaw System — slewing bearing, ring gear,"},{"u":"/en/field-notes/yaw-hunting/","b":"Yaw Hunting — The Nacelle Oscillates While Turning","h":"Preventive maintenance","t":" vane sensor ¶ Case 06: Yaw Bearing Wear — the same system, a different fault ¶ Enercon E92 — model guide ¶ All field notes ¶ Fault diagnosis tree (Turkish)"},{"u":"/en/tools/bolt-tension/","b":"Bolt Tension Calculator — Preload, Tensioner Pressure, Load Cell","h":"","t":"Field tool ¶ Bolt Tension Calculator ¶ Preload calculations for wind turbine blade root bolts, nacelle connections, gearbox flanges and hub joints. Target preload to tensioner pressure, plus the load cell verification that confirms you actually got it. ¶ Bolt diameter M10 (Ø 10 mm) M12 (Ø 12 mm) M16 (Ø 16 mm) M20 (Ø 20 mm) M24 (Ø 24 mm) M30 (Ø 30 mm) ¶ Target preload (kN) 1 kN is about 102 kgf. The recommended limit for the selected diameter is checked automatically. ¶ Target preload ¶ —"},{"u":"/en/tools/bolt-tension/","b":"Bolt Tension Calculator — Preload, Tensioner Pressure, Load Cell","h":"Hydraulic tensioner pressure tables","t":"On a wind turbine, blade root and gearbox flange joints are normally made up with a hydraulic tensioner rather than a torque wrench. These tables give a starting pressure for a target preload."},{"u":"/en/tools/bolt-tension/","b":"Bolt Tension Calculator — Preload, Tensioner Pressure, Load Cell","h":"M20 bolt — grade 8.8 (ISO 898)","t":"Target preload | Tensioner pressure | Air delivery (L/min @ 7 bar) | Estimated time | ¶ 50 kN | 10–12 bar | 15–20 | 5–10 s | ¶ 100 kN | 20–25 bar | 30–40 | 8–12 s | ¶ 150 kN | 30–35 bar | 45–55 | 10–15 s | ¶ 200 kN | 40–45 bar | 60–70 | 12–20 s |"},{"u":"/en/tools/bolt-tension/","b":"Bolt Tension Calculator — Preload, Tensioner Pressure, Load Cell","h":"M24 bolt — grade 10.9","t":"Target preload | Tensioner pressure | Air delivery (L/min @ 7 bar) | Estimated time | ¶ 100 kN | 15–18 bar | 25–30 | 6–10 s | ¶ 150 kN | 22–27 bar | 40–50 | 10–15 s | ¶ 200 kN | 30–35 bar | 55–65 | 12–18 s | ¶ 250 kN | 40–50 bar | 70–85 | 15–25 s | ¶ 300 kN | 55–65 bar | 90–110 | 20–30 s |"},{"u":"/en/tools/bolt-tension/","b":"Bolt Tension Calculator — Preload, Tensioner Pressure, Load Cell","h":"M30 bolt — grade 10.9 (blade root flange)","t":"Target preload | Tensioner pressure | Air delivery (L/min @ 7 bar) | Estimated time | ¶ 200 kN | 20–25 bar | 35–45 | 10–15 s | ¶ 300 kN | 30–40 bar | 55–70 | 15–25 s | ¶ 400 kN | 45–55 bar | 80–100 | 20–35 s | ¶ 500 kN | 60–75 bar | 110–130 | 30–40 s | ¶ Note: tensioner pressure varies with the condition of the tool, ambient temperature and lubrication. These are starting estimates only; verification with a load cell is mandatory."},{"u":"/en/tools/bolt-tension/","b":"Bolt Tension Calculator — Preload, Tensioner Pressure, Load Cell","h":"Load cell verification","t":"After tensioning, the achieved preload is confirmed with a load cell. The protocol: ¶ Load cell (600 t capacity is typical): positioned over the bolt ¶ Baseline: unloaded reading of 0 ± 0.5 kN ¶ Calibrated pull cylinder: lightly load the bolt already tensioned by the tool ¶ Reading: compare against target preload; ±5% is normally acceptable ¶ Documentation: every bolt's load cell reading is recorded ¶ Blade root example: eight M30 bolts, target 250 kN each. Load cell readings 248 kN, 252 kN, 251 kN and so on — all within ±5% of 250 kN, so the joint is accepted."},{"u":"/en/tools/bolt-tension/","b":"Bolt Tension Calculator — Preload, Tensioner Pressure, Load Cell","h":"Preload estimate","t":"T = (C × d × F_y × S) / 1000 ¶ T: preload (kN) ¶ C: tension coefficient (about 0.75 for dry surfaces) ¶ d: bolt diameter (mm) ¶ F_y: yield strength (grade 8.8 = 640 MPa, grade 10.9 = 900 MPa) ¶ S: safety factor, usually 0.75 to stay below 75% of yield"},{"u":"/en/tools/bolt-tension/","b":"Bolt Tension Calculator — Preload, Tensioner Pressure, Load Cell","h":"Rough torque equivalent","t":"Torque (N·m) ≈ K × F (N) × d (m), with K about 0.20 for unlubricated steel. ¶ This is for comparison only. Torque control scatters preload by roughly ±25% because most of the applied torque goes into friction rather than tension, which is exactly why critical joints are tensioned hydraulically and verified with a load cell."},{"u":"/en/tools/bolt-tension/","b":"Bolt Tension Calculator — Preload, Tensioner Pressure, Load Cell","h":"Safety notes","t":"Compressed air system: check the tensioner circuit for leaks weekly. ¶ Over-tensioning: taking a bolt above about 80% of yield risks stress cracking and sudden failure. ¶ Under-tensioning: a joint below target will loosen and fret under vibration. ¶ Star pattern: always tension in a crossing sequence so load distributes evenly around the flange. ¶ Records: all readings must be documented — OEM warranty terms normally require it. ¶ The manufacturer's value governs. Everything on this page is a field aid for estimation and cross-checking. The target preload, tool and sequence for your specific joint come from the OEM documentation. ¶ Related ¶ All calculators ¶ Vestas V136 — blade root bolting on long blades ¶ Gearbox System ¶ Field fault cases"},{"u":"/en/guide/","b":"Becoming a Wind Turbine Technician — A Field Guide","h":"","t":"← Home Field guide ¶ Becoming a wind turbine technician ¶ A lot of people want into this trade, and most of what is written about it online was written by people who have never climbed a tower. I have been at the foot of one since 2013 — Enercon, Nordex, Nordex-Acciona and Vestas machines, erection, commissioning, service and fault finding. What follows is the honest version: what the work is, what it takes to get in, and what it costs you. ¶ 13 years on site300+ turbinesIzmir, TürkiyeUpdated September 2026 ¶ Section 01"},{"u":"/en/guide/","b":"Becoming a Wind Turbine Technician — A Field Guide","h":"Getting into the trade","t":"There is no single door. The people I have worked with came from automotive workshops, from marine engineering, from industrial electrics, from the army, and a few straight out of technical school. What they had in common was not a qualification. It was that somebody was willing to take them up a tower once, and they came down still wanting the job. ¶ If you are starting from nothing, the order that works is: get a mechanical or electrical grounding somewhere — any industry will do — then get your GWO certificates, then apply to erection and installation crews rather than service crews. Erection work is harder on the body and easier to get into. It is also where you learn what the machine is made of, in the most literal way possible, because you bolt it together yourself. ¶ Service work comes after. It pays better, it is more diagnostic than physical, and companies prefer to hire people who have already proven they can work at height without being a liability. ¶ What actually gets you hired: valid GWO certification, no fear of height that you cannot manage, a driving licence, and being able to read a technical drawing and a wiring diagram. English helps enormously — the manuals are in English almost everywhere, whatever the site speaks."},{"u":"/en/guide/","b":"Becoming a Wind Turbine Technician — A Field Guide","h":"The physical requirement is real","t":"You climb 80 to 120 metres carrying tools, in a harness, sometimes several times a week. Most modern towers have a service lift and it does most of the work, but the ladder is the escape route and you have to be able to use it. There is a weight limit on the lift and on the rescue equipment. If you cannot climb the tower in the training tower, you will not be sent to a real one, and that is the right decision. ¶ Section 02"},{"u":"/en/guide/","b":"Becoming a Wind Turbine Technician — A Field Guide","h":"GWO and the certificates that matter","t":"GWO is the Global Wind Organisation. It exists because turbine owners, manufacturers and service companies got tired of every one of them running incompatible safety training, and agreed on a shared standard instead. Its certificates are recognised across manufacturers and across borders, which is exactly what makes them worth having. ¶ The Basic Safety Training package is the entry ticket. It is usually five days and covers: ¶ Working at Heights — harness use, fall arrest, tower climbing and, crucially, rescuing an unconscious colleague from a nacelle. This is the module that decides whether the trade is for you. ¶ First Aid — casualty care in a place an ambulance cannot reach for a long time. ¶ Manual Handling — how to move heavy things in a space with no room to move them. ¶ Fire Awareness — a nacelle fire has no good outcomes; the module is about not having one. ¶ Sea Survival — offshore only, and a separate matter entirely. ¶ Certificates expire after two years and refreshing them is your employer's problem in theory and yours in practice, because a lapsed certificate means you are not allowed on site that morning. Keep your own copies and your own calendar. ¶ Beyond GWO there are manufacturer courses — Vestas SIT, Nordex and Enercon platform training and so on. You do not chase these; you get them because a company sends you, and each one narrows and deepens what you are "},{"u":"/en/guide/","b":"Becoming a Wind Turbine Technician — A Field Guide","h":"A day on site","t":"It starts in the dark more often than not, because you want to be at the turbine when it gets light. There is a toolbox talk: what the job is, what the risks are today, what the wind is doing. Wind is the thing that decides your day. Above a certain speed nobody climbs, and that limit is not negotiable no matter how far you drove to get there. ¶ Then the drive out. Wind farms are on ridgelines and in places without roads, which is the point; it also means an hour of gravel track before you reach the machine. You stop the turbine, lock it out, put the tag on, and climb once — with everything you will need, because going back down for a forgotten tool costs you an hour."},{"u":"/en/guide/","b":"Becoming a Wind Turbine Technician — A Field Guide","h":"What the work looks like once you are up","t":"On a scheduled service you have a checklist and you follow it: torque checks on the flange bolts, oil levels and samples, filter changes, greasing points, brake pad measurements, cleaning the cooling radiators, functional tests on the safety chain. It is methodical work and the checklist exists because memory is unreliable at the end of a ten-hour day. ¶ On a fault call it is different. You arrive with an alarm code and a question. The first thing you do is not open anything — it is read the alarm list in time order and find the first alarm that dropped, because everything after it is usually a consequence. Then the trends: wind, rotor speed, power, pitch angle, temperatures, in the minutes around the event. The machine has already told you most of the story. Your job is to read it before you start turning screws. ¶ The unglamorous part: paperwork. Every job produces a report, and the report is what the customer actually receives. Technicians who write clearly get trusted with more interesting work, and that is not a coincidence. ¶ You come down before dark, drive back, and write it up. Some days you fix it and the machine is producing before you leave, and there is a particular satisfaction in watching the rotor start turning from the bottom of the tower. Some days you find a gearbox that needs a crane and a six-figure decision, and all you can do is document it properly. ¶ Se"},{"u":"/en/guide/","b":"Becoming a Wind Turbine Technician — A Field Guide","h":"How a turbine actually works","t":"The physics fits in one line: the power available in the wind is P = ½ ρ A v³. Half the air density, times the area the rotor sweeps, times wind speed cubed. Everything else in the machine is an attempt to capture as much of that as possible without destroying itself. ¶ The cube is the important part. Ten percent more wind is about thirty-three percent more power, which is why a site with slightly better wind is worth far more than a site with slightly better equipment, and why hub height keeps rising. ¶ The area matters directly, which is why rotors keep growing: a 90 metre rotor sweeps 6,362 square metres, and every extra metre of diameter buys area faster than it costs weight."},{"u":"/en/guide/","b":"Becoming a Wind Turbine Technician — A Field Guide","h":"Why you cannot take all of it","t":"If a rotor extracted every joule from the air, the air behind it would have to stop dead — and then nothing more could pass through. The optimum that resolves that contradiction is the Betz limit: 16/27, or 59.3 percent. Real machines lose more to friction, tip losses and having only three blades, and a good one peaks at a power coefficient of 0.45 to 0.50."},{"u":"/en/guide/","b":"Becoming a Wind Turbine Technician — A Field Guide","h":"The control problem","t":"Below rated wind speed the turbine wants everything it can get, so it varies rotor speed to hold the tip speed ratio near its optimum, around 7 to 9. Above rated wind speed it has all the power it can use, so it pitches the blades to throw the excess away and hold output flat. At the cut-out speed — usually 25 m/s — it feathers the blades and stops, because beyond that the loads cost more than the energy is worth. ¶ If you want to see the machine rather than read about it, there is a 3D model of a Nordex N90 on this site, built to real dimensions and assembled stage by stage from the foundation up. The labels are in Turkish; the geometry is not. ¶ Section 05"},{"u":"/en/guide/","b":"Becoming a Wind Turbine Technician — A Field Guide","h":"The faults you meet first","t":"New technicians expect dramatic failures. What you actually get, most weeks, is small electrical faults: a sensor reading nonsense, a limit switch out of position, a loose terminal that heats up, a cooling fan that will not start. They are quick to fix once found, and finding them is the whole skill. ¶ Pitch systemEncoder drift, drive faults, slip ring contact problems, blade bearing friction. A large share of unplanned stops, and the one system where the backup batteries are a genuine last line of safety. ¶ Yaw systemMotors running too often because the wind vane has drifted, brakes not releasing fully, gear teeth wearing from jerky movement. Watch the daily yaw movement count — a sudden rise is the clearest early warning there is. ¶ GearboxThe expensive one. Temperature alarms are usually a cooling problem first, an oil problem second and a bearing problem third. Oil analysis and vibration data warn long before temperature does. ¶ BladesLeading edge erosion costs production quietly for years. Cracks and delamination are the ones that stop the machine immediately, and the ones you never guess about — you photograph, report and wait for an engineering answer. ¶ The habit that separates good technicians from the rest is refusing to reset an alarm before understanding it. A reset without a cause found buys you a few hours and then the same fault comes back, usually at a worse tim"},{"u":"/en/guide/","b":"Becoming a Wind Turbine Technician — A Field Guide","h":"The faults you meet first","t":"e. ¶ There is an interactive fault diagnosis tree on the Turkish side of this site that walks from symptom to likely cause across ten systems. It is in Turkish for now; if there is interest in an English version, tell me and I will build one. ¶ Section 06"},{"u":"/en/guide/","b":"Becoming a Wind Turbine Technician — A Field Guide","h":"The career, honestly","t":"The good side is real. The industry is growing, the skills transfer between manufacturers and between countries, and experienced technicians are genuinely scarce. After a few years you can move toward commissioning, troubleshooting specialist work, blade inspection, or site and crew management. English and a willingness to travel roughly double the doors that are open to you. ¶ The cost is also real, and people rarely mention it. You work away from home, in blocks. You work in weather that most people watch from indoors. There is callout and shift work. The job stays physical, and bodies do not stay young. A career here is something you plan around rather than drift through. ¶ What I would tell someone starting: learn the electrical side even if you came in mechanical, and the mechanical side even if you came in electrical — the people who can do both are the ones who get the interesting calls. Write good reports. And keep your own record of every machine, every fault and every fix, because after ten years that record is the most valuable thing you own. ¶ If you have a question: I answer everything that is not spam. There is a CV on the home page, and my address is at the bottom of every page here. ¶ Frequently asked"},{"u":"/en/guide/","b":"Becoming a Wind Turbine Technician — A Field Guide","h":"Questions I get","t":"Do I need a degree?No. A technical or vocational background in mechanical or electrical work is the usual route, and plenty of good technicians came from other trades entirely. Valid GWO certificates, a serious attitude to safety and the physical ability to work at height are what decide hiring. The turbine-specific knowledge is taught on the job, because it has to be — no school teaches a particular manufacturer's fleet. ¶ Is GWO training legally mandatory?Not everywhere, as a matter of law. In practice, almost no site will let you climb without it, so the distinction does not help you. Budget for the Basic Safety Training package and for refreshing it every two years. ¶ How high do you actually climb, and how often?Typically 80 to 120 metres, usually once per job. You take everything up with you because a return trip costs an hour. Most modern towers have a single-person service lift that does the climbing, but you must still be able to use the ladder, since that is the escape route. ¶ What happens when it is too windy?Nobody climbs. Every site has a wind speed limit for climbing and for crane work, and it is not negotiable regardless of schedule pressure. You wait, you do ground-level work, or you go home. Anyone who pushes on that is someone to be careful around. ¶ Can I do this job without English?In your own country, yes, and many people do. But the service manuals, alarm"},{"u":"/en/guide/","b":"Becoming a Wind Turbine Technician — A Field Guide","h":"Questions I get","t":" lists and spare part catalogues arrive in English almost everywhere, so without it you are working from someone else's translation of the thing you need to be precise about. It is also the difference between working at home and working anywhere. There is a bilingual glossary on this site aimed exactly at that gap. ¶ What does it pay?It varies far too much by country, employer and whether you are on erection, service or commissioning for a number here to mean anything useful. What is generally true: erection pays less than service, service pays less than commissioning and specialist troubleshooting, working abroad pays more than working at home, and experience on more than one manufacturer's platform is what moves you up fastest. ¶ More"},{"u":"/en/guide/","b":"Becoming a Wind Turbine Technician — A Field Guide","h":"Also on this site","t":"Glossary — 148 terms →Wind turbine terminology in English and Turkish, searchable and grouped by system. Calculators — 6 tools →Tip speed, capacity factor, annual energy production, bolt torque. Formulas shown. N90 3D model →A 2.5 MW turbine assembled stage by stage, built to real dimensions. Türkçe rehber →Aynı konuların Türkçe ve daha ayrıntılı anlatımı. ¶ Contact ¶ Get in touch ¶ sonersoylu@yandex.com Back to the English home page →"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"","t":"← Home Glossary ¶ Wind turbine terms ¶ On site the talking happens in one language and the manual is written in another. This glossary sits in that gap: every term here I have used at the foot of a tower or inside a nacelle, with the Turkish equivalent next to it. The definitions come from the work, not from a dictionary. ¶ 148 termsEnglish – Turkish6 categoriesUpdated September 2026 ¶ Clear All 148Components 47Aerodynamics 33Electrical 23Control 12Maintenance 20Safety 13 ¶ 148 terms shown"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Airfoil","t":"Kanat profili ¶ The cross-sectional shape of the blade. Wind turbines use thick sections from the NACA or DU families. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Anemometer","t":"Anemometre ¶ The wind speed sensor on top of the nacelle. The controller decides cut-in and cut-out from it. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Angle of attack","t":"Hücum açısı ¶ The angle between the relative wind and the blade chord. Too large and the flow separates: the blade stalls. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Automatic lubrication unit","t":"Yağlama ünitesi ¶ The automatic greaser that pumps measured grease into bearings and gear teeth on a schedule. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Availability","t":"Erişilebilirlik ¶ The share of time the turbine is available to produce. Contracts often penalise anything under 97 percent. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Blade","t":"Kanat ¶ An aerodynamic shell of glass or carbon fibre composite. On an N90 it is 43.8 m long and weighs six to seven tonnes on its own. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Bolt tensioning","t":"Gerdirme ¶ Stretching the bolt directly with a hydraulic tensioner and seating the nut. Preferred on large flanges. ¶ Maintenance"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Borescope inspection","t":"Endoskopi ¶ Inspecting inside the gearbox with a camera without opening it. The fastest way to see tooth surface damage. ¶ Maintenance"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Brake pad","t":"Fren balatası ¶ The friction element in the brake caliper. Measuring pad wear is a standard item in every scheduled service. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Capacity factor","t":"Kapasite faktörü ¶ Annual energy produced divided by what the machine would make running flat out all year. Typical onshore values in Türkiye are 30 to 38 percent. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Chord","t":"Veter uzunluğu ¶ The distance from leading edge to trailing edge of a blade section. It tapers from root to tip. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Confined space","t":"Kapalı alan ¶ A space with restricted entry and poor ventilation. The hub and the inside of a blade both count. ¶ Safety"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Cooling unit","t":"Soğutma ünitesi ¶ The radiator and fan set that cools gearbox oil and the generator. One of the biggest sources of summer alarms. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Coupling","t":"Kaplin ¶ The flexible connection between gearbox and generator. It absorbs misalignment and vibration, and slips under overload. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"DFIG","t":"Çift beslemeli asenkron jeneratör ¶ A generator whose rotor winding is fed through a converter. It makes variable speed economical; this is what the N90 uses. ¶ Electrical"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Disc brake","t":"Disk fren ¶ The mechanical brake on the high-speed shaft. It backs up aerodynamic braking and holds the rotor for parking and service. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Emergency stop (E-stop)","t":"Acil durdurma ¶ A stop triggered by a red button, breaking the safety chain directly. The blades go to feather. ¶ Control"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Fault code / alarm code","t":"Alarm kodu ¶ The number the control system raises for a specific fault. Most service work follows that number. ¶ Control"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Feathering / Feather position","t":"Bayrak konumu ¶ Turning the blade roughly 90 degrees so it offers minimum resistance. It is the primary way to stop a turbine. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Flange","t":"Flanş ¶ The ring-shaped face where two sections meet and are bolted together. The most critical place for torque checks. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Foundation","t":"Temel ¶ The reinforced concrete mass that carries the turbine. A machine in the N90 class sits on 500 to 800 m³ of concrete. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Full body harness","t":"Tam vücut emniyet kemeri ¶ The wearer's side of the fall arrest system. It is inspected by eye before every use. ¶ Safety"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Full power converter","t":"Tam güç konvertörü ¶ A converter the whole generator output passes through. It gives the most flexible grid compliance. ¶ Electrical"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Gearbox","t":"Dişli kutusu ¶ The transmission that steps the rotor's roughly 15 rpm up to the 1,500 rpm the generator needs. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Generator","t":"Jeneratör ¶ The machine that turns mechanical rotation into electricity. The N90 uses a doubly-fed induction type. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Global Wind Organisation","t":"GWO ¶ The body that sets the industry's common training standard. Its certificates are effectively mandatory for site access. ¶ Safety"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Harmonics","t":"Harmonik ¶ Distorting current and voltage components at multiples of the fundamental frequency, produced by power electronics. ¶ Electrical"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"High-speed shaft (HSS)","t":"Yüksek hız mili ¶ The shaft between gearbox output and generator. The mechanical disc brake usually sits on it. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Hub","t":"Göbek ¶ The cast structure the blades bolt onto, carried by the main shaft. It houses the pitch drives and their backup batteries. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Icing","t":"Buzlanma ¶ Ice forming on the blade. It causes imbalance, lost output and an ice-throw risk, and calls for a shutdown. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Induction / asynchronous generator","t":"Asenkron jeneratör ¶ A generator whose rotor turns with slip relative to grid frequency. Simple and rugged. ¶ Electrical"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Leading edge erosion","t":"Hücum kenarı erozyonu ¶ Wear of the blade's leading edge from rain and dust. It costs production and is repaired with coating. ¶ Maintenance"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Lightning protection system (LPS)","t":"Yıldırım koruma sistemi ¶ The chain of receptor, conductor and earthing. One of the most frequently measured systems on site. ¶ Electrical"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Lightning receptor","t":"Yıldırım alıcısı ¶ The metal disc at the blade tip. It catches the strike and takes it down a conductor to earth. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Limit switch","t":"Sınır anahtarı ¶ A switch detecting a mechanical end position. It stops pitch and yaw from over-travelling. ¶ Electrical"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Lock-out tag-out (LOTO)","t":"Etiketleme-kilitleme ¶ Cutting the energy and securing it with a physical lock and tag. The first step of any electrical work. ¶ Safety"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Low voltage ride through (LVRT)","t":"Düşük gerilim atlatma ¶ The turbine's ability to stay connected through a short grid voltage dip. It is a grid code requirement. ¶ Electrical"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Main bearing","t":"Ana yatak ¶ The large bearing supporting the main shaft. Replacing it is one of the most expensive failures in the machine. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Major component exchange (MCE)","t":"Büyük bileşen değişimi ¶ Replacing a heavy part such as a gearbox, generator or blade using a crane. A costly operation lasting days. ¶ Maintenance"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Medium voltage (MV)","t":"Orta gerilim ¶ Generally the 6 to 36 kV range. The turbine transformer output and site distribution sit here. ¶ Electrical"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Met mast","t":"Meteoroloji direği ¶ An independent measurement mast on site. It is the reference for power curve verification. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Nacelle","t":"Nasel ¶ The machine house on top of the tower. It holds the main bearing, gearbox, brake, generator and control cabinet. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Permanent magnet generator (PMG)","t":"Sabit mıknatıslı jeneratör ¶ A generator with magnets in the rotor, needing no excitation current. Common on direct drive machines. ¶ Electrical"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Pitch control","t":"Pitch kontrolü ¶ Setting power and loads by rotating the blade about its axis. The standard on modern machines. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Pitch drive","t":"Pitch motoru ¶ The electric (or hydraulic) drive that turns the blade. On loss of supply, batteries drive it to feather. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Pitch system","t":"Pitch sistemi ¶ The system that rotates each blade about its own axis to set the angle of attack and therefore the power. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Planetary stage","t":"Planet kademesi ¶ The high-torque input stage of the gearbox, which spreads load across several planet gears. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"PLC","t":"PLC ¶ The turbine's main control computer. It reads every sensor, drives every actuator and raises the alarms. ¶ Control"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Power coefficient (Cp)","t":"Güç katsayısı ¶ How much of the wind's energy the rotor actually takes. A good machine peaks around 0.45 to 0.50. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Prebend","t":"Prebend ¶ The slight upwind curvature built into the blade. It keeps the tip clear of the tower under load. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Predictive maintenance","t":"Kestirimci bakım ¶ Catching a failure early from vibration, oil and temperature data and acting before it happens. ¶ Maintenance"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Preload","t":"Ön yükleme ¶ The tensile force in the bolt produced by tightening. Preload is what actually holds the joint, not the torque itself. ¶ Maintenance"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"PT100 (RTD)","t":"PT100 ¶ A sensor measuring temperature through resistance change. Standard for bearing and winding temperatures. ¶ Electrical"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Rated wind speed","t":"Anma rüzgâr hızı ¶ The wind speed at which rated power is first reached. Above it, pitch holds power constant. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Reset","t":"Reset ¶ Clearing the alarm and returning the turbine to production. A reset without a cause found will repeat. ¶ Control"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Rotor","t":"Rotor ¶ The rotating assembly of blades, hub and spinner. This is what converts the wind's kinetic energy into rotation. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Safety chain","t":"Güvenlik zinciri ¶ The hardwired circuit with every critical safety contact in series. If any one opens, the turbine stops. ¶ Control"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"SCADA","t":"SCADA ¶ The system for monitoring and commanding every turbine on site remotely. Production, alarm and trend data live here. ¶ Control"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Slip ring","t":"Slip ring ¶ The brush and ring assembly that carries power and signals between the rotating hub and the fixed nacelle. Carbon dust build-up is a classic fault. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Sound power level","t":"Ses gücü seviyesi ¶ The total acoustic power the turbine emits, in dB(A). It can be turned down in a night mode. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Spar cap","t":"Kiriş / spar ¶ The main composite band inside the blade that carries the bending load. The spine of the blade. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Spinner / Nose cone","t":"Burun konisi ¶ The aerodynamic cover over the hub. Everyone calls it the spinner; you climb through it to service the pitch system. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Switchgear","t":"Şalt hücresi ¶ The cabinet holding the medium voltage breaker, disconnector and instrument transformers, at the tower base. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Tilt angle","t":"Tilt açısı ¶ The angle of the main shaft to horizontal, about 5 degrees. It increases blade-to-tower clearance. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Tip speed","t":"Kanat ucu hızı ¶ The linear speed of the blade tip. Onshore it is usually capped near 80 m/s because of noise. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Tip speed ratio (λ)","t":"Kanat ucu hız oranı ¶ The ratio of blade tip speed to wind speed. Modern machines run best between 7 and 9. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Tower","t":"Kule ¶ The tapered steel shell that lifts the rotor to where the wind is strong and steady. Usually erected in three to five sections. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Tower section","t":"Kule segmenti ¶ One piece of the tower, bolted to the next through a flange. Transport limits decide how long a section can be. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Transformer","t":"Trafo ¶ The transformer that steps generator voltage up to medium voltage. It sits at the tower base or inside the nacelle. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Turbulence intensity (TI)","t":"Türbülans yoğunluğu ¶ The variation in wind speed relative to the mean. High turbulence drives fatigue loads and failures. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Twist","t":"Burulma ¶ The twist built into the blade from root to tip. It keeps the angle of attack right at every station. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Vortex generator","t":"Vorteks jeneratörü ¶ Small fins bonded onto the blade. They delay flow separation and can add a few percent of output. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Wake effect","t":"İz etkisi ¶ The slowing and added turbulence downwind of a turbine. It cuts the output of machines behind it. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Weibull distribution","t":"Weibull dağılımı ¶ The statistical model describing how wind speeds are distributed at a site, defined by shape k and scale c. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Wind rose","t":"Rüzgâr gülü ¶ The polar chart of how wind is distributed by direction. Turbine layout is planned from it. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Wind shear","t":"Rüzgâr kayması ¶ The increase of wind speed with height. It means the upper blade sees faster wind than the lower one. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Yaw brake","t":"Yaw freni ¶ The calipers that hold the nacelle once it reaches its target heading. They apply partial pressure while yawing. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Yaw drive","t":"Yaw motoru ¶ The geared electric motor that produces yaw movement. A turbine may carry four to eight of them. ¶ Components"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Yaw misalignment","t":"Yaw sapması ¶ The nacelle sitting off the true wind direction. Even a few degrees costs measurable production. ¶ Aerodynamics"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Yaw system","t":"Yaw sistemi ¶ The system that turns the nacelle into the wind: motors, ring gear, yaw bearing and brakes. ¶ Components ¶ No term matches that search. Try another word — the search looks at the English term, the Turkish equivalent and the definitions. ¶ Frequently asked"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"About these terms","t":"What is a nacelle?The machine house on top of the tower, holding the main bearing, gearbox, brake, generator and control cabinet. It sits on the yaw bearing and turns so the rotor faces the wind. ¶ Pitch or yaw — what is the difference?Pitch is the blade turning about its own axis, which sets the angle of attack and therefore power and load. Yaw is the whole nacelle turning on the tower to face the wind. One turns the blade, the other turns the machine. ¶ Why is this glossary bilingual?Turbine manufacturers are mostly European, so manuals and alarm lists arrive in English while the crew speaks the local language. Anyone working on turbines outside the manufacturer's home country lives in both vocabularies at once. ¶ Something looks wrong or missing.Then write to me. This came out of one technician's experience; other manufacturers and other sites use words differently. If you know a better equivalent, send it with the source and I will add it. ¶ More"},{"u":"/en/glossary/","b":"Wind Turbine Glossary — English / Turkish Terms","h":"Also worth a look","t":"Calculators →Tip speed and TSR, capacity factor, annual energy production, bolt torque. Field guide →Getting into the trade, GWO certificates, a real day on site. 3D turbine model →A 2.5 MW machine assembled stage by stage in your browser. Türkçe sözlük →Aynı sözlüğün Türkçe açıklamalı sürümü. ¶ Contact ¶ Get in touch ¶ sonersoylu@yandex.com Back to the English home page →"},{"u":"/en/tools/","b":"Wind Turbine Calculators — Tip Speed, Capacity Factor, Bolt Torque","h":"","t":"← Home Calculators ¶ Wind turbine calculators ¶ The six calculations I reach for most often, on site and at the desk. Everything runs in your browser: no data leaves the page and nothing asks you to sign up. Under each tool I have written the formula and the assumptions behind it, because where a number comes from matters as much as the number itself. ¶ 6 toolsNo sign-upFormulas shownUpdated September 2026 ¶ Tool 01"},{"u":"/en/tools/","b":"Wind Turbine Calculators — Tip Speed, Capacity Factor, Bolt Torque","h":"Tip speed and TSR","t":"The linear speed of the blade tip drives both noise and leading edge erosion. Onshore it is usually held near 80 m/s because of noise limits. The tip speed ratio (TSR, λ) tells you how many times faster the tip travels than the wind; on three-bladed rotors efficiency peaks between 7 and 9. ¶ Rotor diameter m ¶ Rotor speed rpm ¶ Wind speed m/s ¶ The default values are those of a Nordex N90/2500: 90 m rotor, rated speed 18.1 rpm. ¶ Tip speed—m/s ¶ Per hour—km/h ¶ TSR (λ)— ¶ Angular velocity—rad/s ¶ — ¶ Copy a link to this calculation ¶ ω = 2π · n / 60  ·  vtip = ω · D/2  ·  λ = vtip / vwind ¶ n: revolutions per minute, D: rotor diameter, ω: angular velocity. TSR is dimensionless. ¶ Tool 02"},{"u":"/en/tools/","b":"Wind Turbine Calculators — Tip Speed, Capacity Factor, Bolt Torque","h":"Power in the wind and turbine power","t":"Double the wind speed and power goes up eightfold. That one sentence explains why site selection matters more than anything else in wind energy. Below, air density is calculated from temperature and altitude, so you can see for yourself why the same wind gives you less power on a hot summer day. ¶ Rotor diameterm ¶ Wind speedm/s ¶ Wind speed slider ¶ Air temperature°C ¶ Altitudem ¶ Power coefficient Cp – ¶ A good modern turbine peaks between 0.45 and 0.50. The theoretical ceiling, the Betz limit, is 0.593. ¶ Turbine power (with Cp)—kW ¶ Total power in the wind—kW ¶ Betz limit (59.3%)—kW ¶ Swept area—m² ¶ Power density—W/m² ¶ Air density—kg/m³ ¶ — ¶ Copy a link to this calculation ¶ A = π·D²/4  ·  Pwind = ½ · ρ · A · v³  ·  Pturbine = Pwind · Cp ¶ ρ = p / (R · T)  ·  p = 101325 · (1 − 2.25577·10−5 · h)5.25588 ¶ R = 287.05 J/(kg·K), T absolute temperature (K), h altitude (m). Under standard conditions (15 °C, sea level) ρ = 1.225 kg/m³. ¶ Tool 03"},{"u":"/en/tools/","b":"Wind Turbine Calculators — Tip Speed, Capacity Factor, Bolt Torque","h":"Wind speed correction with height","t":"The met mast sits at 30 metres and the hub at 80. You cannot estimate production without bridging that gap. Wind speeds up with height, and how much depends on the roughness of the terrain. Below you get the power law and the logarithmic law side by side. ¶ Measured speedm/s ¶ Measurement heightm ¶ Target height (hub)m ¶ Terrain type Open sea or lake surface Open flat terrain, short grass Farmland with scattered buildings Scrub, scattered trees and buildings Forest, villages and broken terrain City with tall buildings ¶ The choice sets both the roughness length (z₀) and the power law exponent (α). ¶ Speed at target height—m/s ¶ By the logarithmic law—m/s ¶ Increase in speed—% ¶ Increase in power—% ¶ — ¶ Copy a link to this calculation ¶ Power law: v₂ = v₁ · (h₂ / h₁)α ¶ Logarithmic law: v₂ = v₁ · ln(h₂/z₀) / ln(h₁/z₀) ¶ Because power goes with the cube of speed, the gain in power is (v₂/v₁)³ − 1. The two laws approximate the same terrain differently; results close to each other are a good sign. ¶ Tool 04"},{"u":"/en/tools/","b":"Wind Turbine Calculators — Tip Speed, Capacity Factor, Bolt Torque","h":"Capacity factor","t":"The single number that captures how a plant is really performing. Onshore in Türkiye the typical range is 30 to 38 percent; anything above 40 means an unusually good site. Equivalent full load hours say the same thing in hours, and both are used in the industry. ¶ Rated powerMW ¶ Energy producedMWh ¶ Period One year (8760 hours) Leap year (8784 hours) One month (730 hours) Three months (2190 hours) I will enter it myself ¶ Period lengthhours ¶ Capacity factor—% ¶ Equivalent full load hours—hours ¶ Average output power—kW ¶ Theoretical maximum production—MWh ¶ — ¶ Copy a link to this calculation ¶ CF = E / (P · t)  ·  Equivalent full load hours = E / P ¶ E is energy produced (MWh), P rated power (MW), t the length of the period (hours). Capacity factor should not be confused with availability: even a turbine that never faults will not approach 100 percent, because the wind does not blow at rated speed all year. ¶ Tool 05"},{"u":"/en/tools/","b":"Wind Turbine Calculators — Tip Speed, Capacity Factor, Bolt Torque","h":"Annual energy production (Weibull)","t":"How wind is distributed across a year at a given site is modelled with a Weibull distribution. Enter the mean speed and the shape parameter, and the tool multiplies that distribution by the turbine power curve and integrates it over the year. It is not a substitute for a real feasibility study, but it gets the order of magnitude right. ¶ Turbine Nordex N90/2500 — real power curve Generic model — cubic approximation ¶ Mean wind speed at hub height m/s ¶ Weibull shape parameter k – ¶ k = 2 (Rayleigh) is a reasonable assumption for most onshore sites. Where the wind is steady it rises to 2.5 or 3. ¶ Total losses % ¶ Wake effects, electrical losses, downtime and availability together typically come to 10 to 15 percent. ¶ Rated powerkW ¶ Rated wind speedm/s ¶ Net annual production—MWh ¶ Gross production before losses—MWh ¶ Capacity factor—% ¶ Equivalent full load hours—hours ¶ Weibull scale parameter c—m/s ¶ Approximate households—homes ¶ — ¶ How production is spread across wind speeds ¶ Copy a link to this calculation ¶ f(v) = (k/c)·(v/c)k−1·e−(v/c)k  ·  c = v̄ / Γ(1 + 1/k) ¶ AEP = 8760 · Σ f(v)·P(v)·Δv · (1 − losses) ¶ The sum runs from 0 to 30 m/s in 0.25 m/s steps. The household figure assumes an average domestic consumption of 3,000 kWh per year and is only there to give a sense of scale. ¶ This is not a feasibility study. A real project decision needs at least a year of on-sit"},{"u":"/en/tools/","b":"Wind Turbine Calculators — Tip Speed, Capacity Factor, Bolt Torque","h":"Annual energy production (Weibull)","t":"e measurement, wake modelling against the actual layout, terrain and roughness analysis and an uncertainty budget. Treat the number here as a first estimate of magnitude. ¶ Tool 06"},{"u":"/en/tools/","b":"Wind Turbine Calculators — Tip Speed, Capacity Factor, Bolt Torque","h":"Bolt tightening torque","t":"There are hundreds of bolts in the tower flanges, the blade roots and the yaw bearing, and every one of them has the same job: to hold enough preload. Torque is not the goal, only an indirect route to preload — and a fairly crude one, since most of the applied torque is spent on friction. This tool is a good way to see how much friction changes the answer. ¶ Bolt size M8M10 M12M14 M16M20 M22M24 M27M30 M33M36 M39M42 M48M56 ¶ Property class 8.8 10.9 12.9 ¶ Nut factor K 0.20 — dry, unlubricated 0.16 — lightly oiled 0.13 — grease or MoS₂ 0.10 — well lubricated ¶ Target preload (percentage of yield) % ¶ With torque tightening the common target is 70 percent. Hydraulic tensioning can go to 75 or 90. ¶ Approximate tightening torque—Nm ¶ Target preload—kN ¶ Tensile stress area—mm² ¶ Yield strength—MPa ¶ Approximate breaking load—kN ¶ Torque if tightened dry—Nm ¶ — ¶ Copy a link to this calculation ¶ F = (target %) · As · Rp0.2  ·  T = K · d · F ¶ As is the tensile stress area (ISO 898-1, coarse thread), Rp0.2 the yield strength of the class (for 8.8: 640 MPa at d ≤ 16 mm, 660 MPa above; 10.9: 940 MPa; 12.9: 1100 MPa), d the nominal bolt diameter and K the nut factor. ¶ On site the only value that counts is the torque in the manufacturer's service documentation. What you get here is an approximation: the real friction coefficient depends on the surface coating, the type of lubricant, th"},{"u":"/en/tools/","b":"Wind Turbine Calculators — Tip Speed, Capacity Factor, Bolt Torque","h":"Bolt tightening torque","t":"e state of the washer under the nut and even the air temperature, so the preload achieved at a given torque can scatter by plus or minus 25 percent. On critical joints, hydraulic tensioning or bolt elongation measurement is preferred over torque. Use this for checking and training, not in place of the manufacturer's figure. ¶ Frequently asked"},{"u":"/en/tools/","b":"Wind Turbine Calculators — Tip Speed, Capacity Factor, Bolt Torque","h":"About these calculations","t":"Why does power scale with the cube of wind speed?The mass of air passing the rotor per second is proportional to speed, and the kinetic energy of that air scales with speed squared. Multiply the two and power depends on speed cubed. The practical consequence: a 10 percent difference in wind speed is roughly a 33 percent difference in power. That is why site selection decides so much. ¶ Can the Betz limit be beaten?No. If the rotor took all the energy out of the wind, the air behind it would have to stop, and then nothing could pass through. The optimum that resolves that contradiction is 59.3 percent. Real turbines sit below it because of friction, tip losses and a finite number of blades, peaking at a Cp of 0.45 to 0.50 at best. ¶ Is capacity factor the same as availability?No, though they are often confused. Availability is the share of time the turbine is ready to produce, and on a well-run site it is above 97 percent. Capacity factor depends on how much the wind blows and sits around 30 to 38 percent. A turbine that never faults all year still will not raise its capacity factor, because the wind does not blow at rated speed. ¶ How do I know the Weibull k for my site?Only measurement gives you the real figure. Without it, k = 2 (the Rayleigh distribution) is a reasonable starting point onshore. Sites with steady wind from a settled direction run at 2.5 to 3; very variable si"},{"u":"/en/tools/","b":"Wind Turbine Calculators — Tip Speed, Capacity Factor, Bolt Torque","h":"About these calculations","t":"tes at 1.5 to 1.8. As k rises the distribution narrows and, at the same mean speed, production falls slightly, because most of the energy comes from speeds above the mean. ¶ Is anything I type here stored?No. Every calculation runs inside your browser and nothing is sent to a server. Close the page and your inputs are gone. ¶ More"},{"u":"/en/tools/","b":"Wind Turbine Calculators — Tip Speed, Capacity Factor, Bolt Torque","h":"Also worth a look","t":"Glossary →148 wind turbine terms in English and Turkish with practical definitions. Bolt tension calculator →Target preload, hydraulic tensioner pressure and load cell verification for blade root and flange joints. Turbine systems →Pitch, yaw, gearbox and generator: how they work and what their normal values are. Field fault cases →Step-by-step diagnosis notes from real faults. Field guide →Getting into the trade, GWO certificates, a real day on site. N90 3D model →See the machine these calculations are built around, assembled stage by stage. Türkçe sürüm →Aynı araçların Türkçe açıklamalı hali. ¶ Contact ¶ Get in touch ¶ sonersoylu@yandex.com Back to the home page →"}]
