+ <p class="kb-kicker">Model guide</p>
+ <h1>Enercon E-70 E4</h1>
+ <p class="kb-lead">Enercon’s gearless 2 MW-class model: 2.3 MW rated power, 71 m rotor. The rotor drives a multi-pole synchronous annular generator directly; there is no gearbox in between. In the field, attention moves from the gearbox to the generator, the pitch system and the converter.</p>
+ <h2>What defines this model</h2>
+ <p>In the Enercon design the inner ring of the generator turns together with the rotor. Speed is low (6–21 rpm), so the generator is multi-pole and large in diameter. The variable-frequency power is rectified and converted to grid frequency by the Enercon converter.</p>
+ <p>Without a gearbox, gear oil changes, oil sampling and gearbox bearing failures drop off the list. Generator cooling, the pitch system’s emergency supply and the converter modules take their place.</p>
+ <h2>Key technical data</h2>
+ <div class="kb-table-scroll"><table>
+  <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Value</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>Rated power</td><td>2.3 MW</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Rotor diameter</td><td>71 m</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Hub height</td><td>64–113 m</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Rotor speed</td><td>6–21 rpm (variable)</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Cut-in</td><td>2.5 m/s</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Storm control</td><td>Power reduced gradually between 28 and 34 m/s</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Generator</td><td>Direct-drive synchronous annular generator</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Grid feed</td><td>Enercon converter (rectifier, DC link, inverter)</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Pitch</td><td>Independent system per blade with emergency supply</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Brakes</td><td>Aerodynamic (pitch), rotor brake, rotor lock</td></tr>
+ </table></div>
+ <h2>Drivetrain and systems</h2>
+ <h3>Annular generator</h3>
+ <ul><li>Large-diameter and multi-pole; heat and clean air paths are the most critical periodic items in this design. On dusty and salty sites a clogged air path comes straight back as a temperature warning.</li></ul>
+ <h3>Storm control</h3>
+ <ul><li>Classic designs stop the turbine completely at a set speed. Enercon reduces speed and power gradually as the wind rises, so the turbine keeps producing partially in high wind.</li><li>Low power in high wind can look like a fault; the control system may simply be working as designed.</li></ul>
+ <h3>Pitch and emergency supply</h3>
+ <ul><li>Each blade has its own pitch system and emergency energy source. The capacity test of that source, which feathers the blades on grid loss, should never be skipped.</li></ul>
+ <h2>E-70 and E-82 (Enercon)</h2>
+ <div class="kb-table-scroll"><table>
+  <tr><th>Item</th><th>E-70 E4</th><th>E-82 E2</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>Rated power</td><td>2.3 MW</td><td>2.0 / 2.3 MW</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Rotor diameter</td><td>71 m</td><td>82 m</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Rotor speed</td><td>6–21 rpm</td><td>6–19.5 rpm</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Drivetrain</td><td>Gearless, annular generator</td><td>Gearless, annular generator</td></tr>
+ </table></div>
+ <h2>Maintenance topics that matter in the field</h2>
+ <ul><li>A slow rise in generator temperature: check the cooling air paths and filters first.</li><li>Pitch emergency supply: capacity test and connections.</li><li>Yaw: adjustment gears and lubrication; the first place to look for hunting and noise.</li><li>Converter modules and cooling: read temperature and fault logs together under load.</li></ul>
+ <aside class="kb-note">
+  <p>About this page</p>
+  <p>Technical data come from documents published by the manufacturer and can change with variant and site configuration. The maintenance topics summarise general field practice; for intervals and torque values the manufacturer’s service documentation governs. My first role at Enercon (2013–2016) was steel–concrete hybrid tower assembly; this page is a model summary built on verified data, not a maintenance technician’s personal log.</p>
+  <p class="kb-author"><span class="who">Soner Soylu</span><span class="role">Wind Turbine Field Service Technician — Enercon, Nordex, Vestas · GWO certified</span></p>
+ </aside>
