   <h3>Is a gearless (direct-drive) turbine better?</h3>
   <p>For maintenance, yes: with no gearbox there is no gear wear, no oil change and no reduction-stage failure. In exchange the annular generator is very heavy, which makes transport and erection harder and raises the initial cost, and generator thermal management becomes the main thing you watch.</p>
   <h3>How long does it take to erect a wind turbine?</h3>
-  <p>It depends heavily on site conditions and the crane plan. The taller the tower and the longer the blade, the longer it takes: roughly 12–14 days for a lighter gearless machine, 16–18 days for a 3 MW geared model, and above 20 days for large-rotor 4 MW machines. Days lost waiting out the wind limit are on top of that.</p>
+  <p>It varies a lot with site conditions, tower type and the crane plan, so a single number would mislead. What drives it most is the number and height of tower sections, blade length, crane rig-up and the days lost waiting out the wind limit. On sites with concrete or hybrid towers, building the tower is a separate job on top.</p>
