+ <p class="kb-kicker">Model guide</p>
+ <h1>Vestas V110-2.0 MW</h1>
+ <p class="kb-lead">The large-rotor member of Vestas’ 2 MW platform for low-wind sites: 2.0 MW rated power, 110 m rotor, 54 m blades. A classic geared architecture with a doubly-fed (slip-ring) generator; in the field most of the time goes into the gearbox oil system, the hydraulic pitch and the generator slip-ring side.</p>
+ <h2>What defines this model</h2>
+ <p>The V110 grows the rotor instead of the rating. A 2.0 MW ceiling on a 110 m rotor gives a turbine that reaches useful power early in light wind and targets a high capacity factor. Wind class IEC IIIA.</p>
+ <p>The architecture is familiar: rotor → main shaft → gearbox → high-speed shaft → doubly-fed generator. The stator connects directly to the grid, the rotor through slip rings to a partial-power converter. Each blade is pitched by its own hydraulic cylinder.</p>
+ <h2>Key technical data</h2>
+ <div class="kb-table-scroll"><table>
+  <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Value</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>Rated power</td><td>2.0 MW</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Rotor diameter</td><td>110 m</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Blade length</td><td>54 m</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Hub height</td><td>80 / 95 / 125 m (by grid frequency and package)</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Cut-in</td><td>3 m/s</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Rated wind speed</td><td>11.5 m/s</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Cut-out</td><td>20 m/s</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Wind class</td><td>IEC IIIA</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Gearbox</td><td>1 planetary + 2 helical stages</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Generator</td><td>Doubly-fed with slip rings; 4-pole (50 Hz)</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Pitch</td><td>Full-blade feathering, one pitch cylinder per blade</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Tower</td><td>Tubular steel</td></tr>
+ </table></div>
+ <h2>Drivetrain and systems</h2>
+ <h3>Gearbox and oil system</h3>
+ <ul><li>One planetary and two helical stages step the main-shaft speed up to generator speed.</li><li>What gets checked most: oil level and temperature trend, filter differential pressure, oil samples (metals and water), seals, and vibration trends for the bearings.</li><li>A temperature that drifts up at the same wind speed matters more than any single absolute value.</li></ul>
+ <h3>Doubly-fed generator (DFIG)</h3>
+ <ul><li>The rotor winding reaches the converter through slip rings and brushes, so brush wear, brush dust and slip-ring surface are periodic checks.</li><li>Because the stator is tied directly to the grid, grid events (voltage dips, imbalance) can come back as generator or converter faults.</li></ul>
+ <h3>Hydraulic pitch</h3>
+ <ul><li>A pitch cylinder turns each blade; pressure, accumulator pre-charge and hose and fitting tightness decide the health of the system.</li><li>The accumulators hold the energy to feather the blades on grid loss; testing them is a safety item that should never be skipped.</li></ul>
+ <h2>V110 and V126 (Vestas)</h2>
+ <div class="kb-table-scroll"><table>
+  <tr><th>Item</th><th>V110-2.0 MW</th><th>V126-3.45 MW</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>Rated power</td><td>2.0 MW</td><td>3.45 MW</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Rotor diameter</td><td>110 m</td><td>126 m</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Gearbox</td><td>1 planetary + 2 helical</td><td>2 planetary + 1 helical</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Generator / converter</td><td>Doubly-fed, partial converter</td><td>Full-scale converter</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Pitch</td><td>Hydraulic cylinder</td><td>Hydraulic cylinder</td></tr>
+ </table></div>
+ <h2>Maintenance topics that matter in the field</h2>
+ <ul><li>Hydraulic leaks in the hub and nacelle: a small drip becomes a dirty hub and an out-of-spec accumulator over time.</li><li>A slow rise in gearbox oil temperature: filter, cooler radiator and oil level are checked together.</li><li>Brush dust in the generator slip-ring compartment: cleaning and brush-length measurement are routine.</li><li>Yaw brake pads and gear lubrication: the first place to look for hunting and noise complaints.</li></ul>
+ <aside class="kb-note">
+  <p>About this page</p>
+  <p>Technical data come from documents published by the manufacturer and can change with variant, software package and site configuration. The maintenance topics summarise general field practice; for intervals and torque values the manufacturer’s service documentation always governs.</p>
+  <p class="kb-author"><span class="who">Soner Soylu</span><span class="role">Wind Turbine Field Service Technician — Enercon, Nordex, Vestas · GWO certified</span></p>
+ </aside>
