+ <p class="kb-kicker">Model guide</p>
+ <h1>Vestas V136</h1>
+ <p class="kb-lead">The large-rotor, low-to-medium-wind model of Vestas’ 4 MW platform: 136 m rotor, 66.7 m blades. The first common version was 3.45 MW, the later one 4.0–4.2 MW. In 2020 I worked on ground and nacelle assembly on a V136-3.45 MW project; the field notes on this page come from that job.</p>
+ <h2>What defines this model</h2>
+ <p>The energy a turbine captures grows linearly with swept area. The V136’s 14,527 m² is about 17% more than the V126 — on a weak-wind site that area is the biggest lever you have.</p>
+ <p>The price is paid in the blade: a 66.7 m blade is long, heavy and flexible. Root moment, blade-bearing load, transport and the crane plan all grow with it. The drivetrain follows the rest of the platform: a gearbox with two planetary and one helical stage and a full-scale converter.</p>
+ <h2>Key technical data</h2>
+ <div class="kb-table-scroll"><table>
+  <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Value</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>Rated power</td><td>3.45 MW / 4.0–4.2 MW (by version)</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Rotor diameter</td><td>136 m</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Swept area</td><td>14,527 m²</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Blade length</td><td>66.7 m</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Hub height (4.2 MW)</td><td>105 / 112 / 117 / 149 / 166 m</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Cut-in</td><td>3 m/s</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Cut-out (4.2 MW)</td><td>25 m/s</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Wind class (4.2 MW)</td><td>IEC IIB / IEC S</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Gearbox</td><td>2 planetary + 1 helical stage</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Converter</td><td>Full scale</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Power regulation</td><td>Pitch regulated, variable speed</td></tr>
+ </table></div>
+ <h2>Drivetrain and systems</h2>
+ <h3>Blade and blade bearing</h3>
+ <ul><li>On a long blade the pre-tension of root bolts and blade-bearing lubrication head the periodic work.</li><li>Even a few degrees of pitch difference between blades comes back as rotor imbalance and vibration; the physical angle is compared with the encoder value.</li></ul>
+ <h3>Assembly</h3>
+ <ul><li>Nacelle and blade lift windows are very sensitive to wind limits; it is usually the hours spent waiting on wind, not the mechanical work, that sets the assembly schedule.</li><li>Torque and tensioning sequences on flange and root connections follow the documentation and are marked; later checks rely on those marks.</li></ul>
+ <h3>Full-scale converter and cooling</h3>
+ <ul><li>All the power passes through the converter; its liquid cooling circuit and power connections are critical maintenance items.</li></ul>
+ <h2>V126 and V136 (Vestas)</h2>
+ <div class="kb-table-scroll"><table>
+  <tr><th>Item</th><th>V126-3.45 MW</th><th>V136-4.2 MW</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>Rotor diameter</td><td>126 m</td><td>136 m</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Swept area</td><td>12,469 m²</td><td>14,527 m²</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Blade length</td><td>—</td><td>66.7 m</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Converter</td><td>Full scale</td><td>Full scale</td></tr>
+ </table></div>
+ <h2>Maintenance topics that matter in the field</h2>
+ <ul><li>On a vibration complaint, verify the pitch angles physically first; look at calibration before blaming the blade.</li><li>Blade-to-tower clearance and blade surface: periodic visual checks matter more on a long blade.</li><li>Converter and gearbox coolers: on a dusty site, radiator cleaning is one of the highest-return jobs.</li></ul>
+ <aside class="kb-note">
+  <p>About this page</p>
+  <p>Technical data come from documents published by the manufacturer; hub heights, wind class and cut-out differ between the 3.45 MW and 4.2 MW versions. For intervals and torque values the manufacturer’s service documentation governs.</p>
+  <p class="kb-author"><span class="who">Soner Soylu</span><span class="role">Wind Turbine Field Service Technician — Enercon, Nordex, Vestas · GWO certified</span></p>
+ </aside>
+ <h2>Sources</h2>
+ <ul class="kb-kaynak"><li><a href="https://www.vestas.com/en/energy-solutions/onshore-wind-turbines/4-mw-platform/V136-4-2-MW" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vestas — V136-4.2 MW ürün sayfası</a></li></ul>
+ <div class="kb-callout"><p><strong>Warning:</strong> Every job in the nacelle, hub and tower follows lockout–tagout (LOTO), rotor lock and the manufacturer’s safety instructions. This page is field experience and published data; it does not replace the service documentation.</p></div>
